"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"][1]={"university_mathematics_en:M1-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M1-L1 – University: Sets, propositional logic and reasoning with proofs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · mathematical\nfoundations<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>At university, mathematics becomes a language of precise statements.\nIt is not enough for a formula to work in a few examples: we want to\nknow <strong>which objects<\/strong> it applies to and\n<strong>why<\/strong> it must hold. For this, we need sets as collections\nof objects, logic to connect statements and proofs as chains of\njustified deductions. The initial aim of this lesson is to read such\ntexts, not to construct difficult proofs independently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>In programming, you are familiar with collections of data, conditions\nand type specifications. Mathematical logic adds a crucial requirement:\nevery statement must make clear which objects are permitted, what claim\nis being made and which assumptions apply. A\n<strong>counterexample<\/strong> can disprove a universal claim. A\n<strong>proof<\/strong>, by contrast, explains why it holds in every\npermitted case.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – be sure to read these before the formulas<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other use or potential pitfall<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{…}<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Curly braces<\/td>\n<td>Enclose the elements of a set<\/td>\n<td>Also used for blocks and objects in programs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∈<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\in<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is an element of’<\/td>\n<td>An object belongs to a set<\/td>\n<td>Not the same as a subset<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∉<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\notin<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is not an element of’<\/td>\n<td>An object does not belong to the set<\/td>\n<td>Negation of membership<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊆<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\subseteq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is a subset of’<\/td>\n<td>All elements on the left also occur on the right; equality\nallowed<\/td>\n<td>Conventions for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊂<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\subset<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsometimes differ<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊂<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\subset<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is a proper subset of’ in this course<\/td>\n<td>A subset, but not identical<\/td>\n<td>In some textbooks\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊂<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\subset<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso denotes a non-strict subset<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∪<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cup<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘union’<\/td>\n<td>Elements in the first or second set or both<\/td>\n<td>Logical ‘or’ for sets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∩<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cap<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘intersection’<\/td>\n<td>Elements in both sets<\/td>\n<td>For events, joint occurrence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⌀<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varnothing<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘empty set’<\/td>\n<td>A set with no elements<\/td>\n<td>Not the same as the number zero<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Z}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Natural numbers, integers, real numbers<\/td>\n<td>Number systems from School Mathematics M1-L1<\/td>\n<td>Whether\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\in\\mathbb{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on convention<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⇒<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Rightarrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘implies’<\/td>\n<td>Direction of inference from assumption to conclusion<\/td>\n<td>Not automatically reversible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⇔<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Leftrightarrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘if and only if’ / ‘equivalent’<\/td>\n<td>Both directions hold<\/td>\n<td>Not merely ordinary equality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∧<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\land<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘and’<\/td>\n<td>Both statements are true<\/td>\n<td><code>&amp;&amp;<\/code> in many programming languages<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘or’<\/td>\n<td>At least one of the statements is true<\/td>\n<td>Usually inclusive or<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>¬<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘not’<\/td>\n<td>Negation of a statement<\/td>\n<td><code>!<\/code> in many programming languages<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∀<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘for all’<\/td>\n<td>The statement holds for every object in the specified domain<\/td>\n<td>Universal quantifier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∃<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘there exists’<\/td>\n<td>At least one object satisfies the statement<\/td>\n<td>Existential quantifier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘there exists exactly one’<\/td>\n<td>Unique existence<\/td>\n<td>Do not confuse with the factorial symbol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>:<\/code> after a set variable<\/td>\n<td>‘with the property’ / ‘such that’<\/td>\n<td>Separates the domain of selection from the condition<\/td>\n<td>In other contexts, division or ratio<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘en’, ‘kay’, ‘em’<\/td>\n<td>Integer indices or counting numbers in the proof<\/td>\n<td>Other variables are also possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘pee’, ‘cue’, ‘ar’; ‘pee of ex’<\/td>\n<td>Statements;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a statement about x<\/td>\n<td>In probability theory,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso stands for probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sets: what belongs to them?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>set<\/strong> is a collection of clearly distinguishable\nobjects, its <strong>elements<\/strong>. Order and repetition do not\nchange the set.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_001_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Venn diagrams for union, intersection and set difference\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Venn diagrams illustrate the union\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cup B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintersection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cap B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>\\<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\setminus B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∉<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\{2,4,6\\}\\\\\n2&amp;\\in A\\\\\n5&amp;\\notin A\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The set A contains the numbers two,\nfour and six. Two is an element of A. Five is not an element of A.’\nObjects can be numbers, strings, functions or even other sets.<\/p>\n<p>Example of set operations:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\{1,2,3\\},\\quad B=\\{3,4\\}\\\\\nA\\cap B&amp;=\\{3\\}\\\\\nA\\cup B&amp;=\\{1,2,3,4\\}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The intersection of A and B contains\nthree; the union contains all numbers that occur in at least one of the\ntwo sets.’ Sets can also be defined by properties rather than by listing\nall their elements.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{Z}:x&gt;0\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘M is the set of all integers x such\nthat x is greater than zero.’ The curly braces describe the set; the\ncolon here means ‘with the property’. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains all <strong>positive<\/strong> integers, not zero or negative\nnumbers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\in A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to <strong>a single element<\/strong>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>⊆<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{2\\}\\subseteq A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to <strong>a set<\/strong> that as a whole is a subset of A. These\ntwo statements are not interchangeable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logical statements and connectives<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematical statement is a sentence that – within the chosen model\nand with defined terms – is either true or false. ‘This number is large’\nis too vague without a definition. ‘This number is greater than ten’, by\ncontrast, can be checked for a given numerical value.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(P\\land Q)\\implies R\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘If P and Q are both true, then R\nfollows.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote statements, not necessarily numbers. An implication does not mean\nthat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\Rightarrow(P\\land Q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso holds. In <strong>classical propositional logic<\/strong>, an\nimplication ‘if P, then Q’ is false precisely when P is true and Q is\nfalse. It asserts neither a temporal sequence nor causation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> If a download is complete\n<strong>and<\/strong> the checksum matches, a program should mark the\nfile as technically consistent. The real application requires additional\nsecurity assumptions; mathematically, however, the condition describes\nan unambiguous logical test.<\/p>\n<p>The logical expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\lor Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses <strong>inclusive or<\/strong>: it is also true when both statements\nare true.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘not P’. <strong>Counterexample to the converse:<\/strong> ‘If an\ninteger is divisible by four, then it is even’ does <strong>not<\/strong>\nimply that every even number is divisible by four. The number six is\neven but not divisible by four.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quantifiers: does this always hold or at least once?<\/h2>\n<p>The words ‘all’, ‘at least one’ and ‘exactly one’ are crucial in\nmathematics. Their position and order alter the meaning of a\nstatement.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x^2\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘For every real number x, x squared is\ngreater than or equal to zero.’ This statement holds regardless of which\nreal number we choose.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x^2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘There exists at least one real number\nx whose square is four.’ In fact, there are two possible values here,\nnamely two and minus two. The existential quantifier\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∃<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes <strong>not<\/strong> say that the solution is unique. There is a\nseparate symbol for that,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Order matters:<\/strong> ‘For every user there is a password’\nis not the same statement as ‘There is a password for all users’.\nMathematics makes this difference visible through the order of the\nquantifiers. <strong>Concrete countercheck:<\/strong> For every number x\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1,2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere is a number y in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1,2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nBut there is <strong>no single<\/strong> number y in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1,2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat equals both one and two. ‘For all x there is a y’ and ‘There is a y\nfor all x’ must therefore not be interchanged.<\/p>\n<p>An important negation rule expressed in words is: ‘Not everyone has\nthe property’ means ‘There is at least one counterexample’. By contrast,\n‘No one has the property’ means something stronger.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>¬<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⇔<\/mo><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\neg(\\forall x\\in M:\\ P(x))\\iff\\exists x\\in M:\\ \\neg P(x)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘It is not true that every element x\nof the set M has property P if and only if there is at least one element\nx that does not have property P.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere means that statement P holds for object x. The rule is valid in\nordinary classical propositional logic. Within the same framework, the\nother negation also holds: ‘There is <strong>no<\/strong> x with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>’\nis equivalent to ‘<strong>All<\/strong> x fail to satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>’.\nOn the <strong>empty set<\/strong>, a universal statement (for all\nelements) is true, whereas an existential statement (there is at least\none) is false; there is no counterexample to the universal statement\nthere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Relations and functions: connections between objects<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>relation<\/strong> describes which objects have a specified\nrelationship, for example ‘is less than’. A <strong>function<\/strong>\nassigns <strong>exactly one<\/strong> output element to every permitted\ninput element. Different inputs may have the same output value; the\nconcept of a function does not automatically require invertibility.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;f\\colon A\\to B\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘f is a mapping from A to B.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the domain and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe codomain. The arrow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere denotes a <strong>mapping between sets<\/strong>, not the limiting\nprocess from School Mathematics M2-L2. The actual image may be smaller\nthan B.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith real numbers as inputs and real numbers as the codomain is a valid\nfunction. The inputs two and minus two both produce four. On this domain\nthere is therefore no unique inverse function, although every input has\nexactly one function value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is a proof?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>definition<\/strong> establishes what a word means. A\n<strong>theorem<\/strong> asserts a conclusion under specified\nassumptions. A <strong>proof<\/strong> shows, using traceable rules of\ninference, that the conclusion follows from the assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Direct proof, as a principle:<\/strong> The sum of two even\nintegers is even. Every even integer can be written as twice an integer.\nFor any two integers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe even starting numbers are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntheir sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2m+2k=2(m+k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m+k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis also an integer, we again obtain twice an integer. Thus the sum is\neven – not just in individual examples we have tested, but in\ngeneral.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>counterexample<\/strong> is sufficient to disprove the claim\n‘All prime numbers are odd’: the number two is an even prime. Many\nconfirming examples, by contrast, do not constitute a complete proof of\na universal statement.<\/p>\n<p>Further proof principles that we will encounter again later:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Contradiction:<\/strong> Temporarily assume the opposite and\nshow that it is logically impossible.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Contraposition:<\/strong> Instead of ‘If P, then Q’, prove\nthe equivalent direction ‘If not Q, then not P’.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Mathematical induction:<\/strong> Prove a base case and show\nthat validity for one index implies validity for the next.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mathematical induction in an accessible example<\/h2>\n<p>We consider the sum of the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npositive integers.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a positive natural number;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a counting variable. The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘capital sigma’) from School Mathematics M2-L16 denotes the addition of\nindividual contributions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘One plus two and so on up to and\nincluding n equals <strong>n times the bracket n plus one, with that\nentire product divided by two<\/strong>.’ The right-hand side is a\ncompact formula for the sum on the left.<\/p>\n<p>In the <strong>base case<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe left-hand side is one and the right-hand side is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1\\cdot 2}{2}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the <strong>induction step<\/strong>, assume that the statement holds\nfor an arbitrary positive number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf we add\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto both sides, we obtain on the right:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+(n+1)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The product of n and the bracket n plus\none, divided by two, and then plus the bracket n plus one, equals the\nproduct of the brackets n plus one and n plus two, all divided by two.’\nAlgebraically, we first factor out\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n+1)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\right)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe right-hand side is precisely the claimed formula for the next number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Logical conclusion:<\/strong> Since the base case at one holds\nand for every positive integer validity for n implies validity for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe formula holds for <strong>all positive natural numbers<\/strong>.\nThis is more than checking many numbers on a computer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Logic and sets are the foundation of type systems, formal\nspecifications, database queries and algorithm verification. Quantifiers\ndistinguish precise statements such as ‘Every input has an output’ from\nstronger claims such as ‘One output is suitable for every input’. This\nlanguage also helps distinguish permitted states, preconditions and\nverifiable guarantees in AI agents.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> Mathematical certainty applies only\nwithin the established definitions and assumptions. A correctly proved\ntheorem about a simplified model does not automatically prove that the\nreal world satisfies all the assumptions of that model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The statements about set membership and quantifiers presuppose a\n<strong>specified universe of discourse<\/strong> and unambiguous\ndefinitions. The truth value of a quantified statement can change if its\ndomain changes; an apparently general statement without a specified\ndomain can therefore be ambiguous. The negation rules here are\nformulated in <strong>classical logic<\/strong>; other logical systems\nmay require additional distinctions. A formula that holds in individual\nexamples does not prove a statement about every element of its\ndomain.<\/p>\n<p>The mathematical induction shown here proves a formula for\n<strong>positive natural numbers<\/strong>. It cannot be transferred to\nany arbitrary set without additional structure. When applying this to\nsoftware, we also distinguish the correctness of a formally proved\nspecification from whether that specification fully reflects real\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to M1-L2<\/h2>\n<p>In M1-L2 we apply this language to <strong>sequences and\nseries<\/strong>. There we see how the familiar phrase ‘The values get\ncloser and closer to a limit’ is translated into a precise statement\nusing quantifiers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M1-L2 – University: Analysis I – Sequences, series and convergence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · foundations of\nanalysis<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>An algorithm can keep calculating new approximations without ever\nreaching an exact ‘final’ step. In analysis, we ask whether such values\nnevertheless approach a fixed value and what ‘arbitrarily accurate’\nreally means. A <strong>sequence<\/strong> is an ordered list of numbers\nthat can theoretically continue indefinitely. A <strong>series<\/strong>\narises when we add the terms of a sequence one after another. The\nconcept of a limit connects these ideas and allows us to distinguish\nfinite calculations from idealised infinite processes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a routine that halves the distance to a target after each\niteration. The remaining distance becomes smaller: one half, one\nquarter, one eighth and so on. Although every individual value remains\npositive, there is no fixed positive threshold below which the sequence\nwould eventually be unable to decrease further. This precise form of\napproach is called <strong>convergence<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read these first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other use<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘a sub n’<\/td>\n<td>nth term of a sequence<\/td>\n<td>More generally, the index can denote a measured value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a_n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘the sequence a sub n’<\/td>\n<td>The entire ordered sequence<\/td>\n<td>Brackets can also group expressions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\in\\mathbb{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n is an element of the natural numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Running positive index; starting point is specified<\/td>\n<td>In some definitions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarts at zero<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n tends to infinity’<\/td>\n<td>The index grows without a fixed upper bound<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a real number to substitute here<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘limit’<\/td>\n<td>Limit<\/td>\n<td>Also used for limits of functions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘el’<\/td>\n<td>Possible limit<\/td>\n<td>Any other parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘epsilon’<\/td>\n<td>An arbitrary, strictly positive degree of accuracy<\/td>\n<td>Error tolerance in applications<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital N’<\/td>\n<td>Threshold for the sequence index<\/td>\n<td>Often also the set of natural numbers in a different typeface<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∀<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n/\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∃<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘for all’ / ‘there exists’<\/td>\n<td>Quantifiers from M1-L1<\/td>\n<td>Formal logic in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert a_n-L\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘absolute value of a sub n minus L’<\/td>\n<td>Distance between the sequence term and the target value<\/td>\n<td>Absolute value has a different geometric interpretation for complex\nnumbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>&lt;<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&lt;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘less than’<\/td>\n<td>Strict bound<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≤<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\leq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nallows equality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘capital sigma’<\/td>\n<td>Sum of terms of a series<\/td>\n<td>Summation symbol in statistics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘k starts at one’<\/td>\n<td>Starting index of the addition<\/td>\n<td>Other index bounds are possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘s sub n’<\/td>\n<td>nth partial sum<\/td>\n<td>Other sequence notation is possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘cue’<\/td>\n<td>Constant factor of a geometric sequence<\/td>\n<td>Any model parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘absolute value of q’<\/td>\n<td>Positive distance from zero<\/td>\n<td>Absolute value in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘sum’<\/td>\n<td>Typographical form of capital sigma<\/td>\n<td>Identical in meaning to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>↛<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘does not converge to’<\/td>\n<td>Negates the stated limit<\/td>\n<td>Not necessarily the only notation for divergence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sequences: numbers with a running index<\/h2>\n<p>A sequence assigns exactly one numerical value to every permitted\ninteger index. It can be regarded as a function whose domain consists of\nnatural numbers.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=\\frac1n\\qquad\\text{for }n=1,2,3,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘a sub n is equal to one divided by n,\nfor n equal to one, two, three and so on.’ The first terms are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs the indices become larger, the value approaches zero.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The limit of a sub n as n tends to\ninfinity is zero.’ A limit does <strong>not have to be a term of the\nsequence<\/strong>. In this example, zero is never attained as a term,\nbut the sequence approaches it arbitrarily closely.<\/p>\n<p>A sequence can be <strong>bounded<\/strong> without converging. The\nalternating sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,-1,1,-1,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example, always remains between minus one and one but has no limit.\nA sequence can also grow without bound:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexceeds every specified real bound; it does not converge to a finite\nreal number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What exactly does ‘arbitrarily close’ mean?<\/h2>\n<p>The mathematical word <strong>convergence<\/strong> does not simply\nmean ‘usually gets smaller’. It requires the terms <strong>to remain\nwithin every positive distance from the target value, however small,\nfrom a sufficiently late index onwards<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>First, the terminology:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘epsilon’) is an arbitrary permitted degree of accuracy, for example\none hundredth.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘capital N’) is a corresponding starting index.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘n is greater than or equal to capital N’) means that we consider all\nsubsequent terms from this point onwards.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℕ<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall\\varepsilon&gt;0\\quad\\exists N\\in\\mathbb{N}\\quad\\forall n\\geq N:\\ \\lvert a_n-L\\rvert&lt;\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘For every positive number epsilon,\nthere exists a natural-number index N such that, for all\n<strong>natural-number<\/strong> indices n from N onwards, the distance\nbetween a sub n and the limit L is less than epsilon.’ Here the terms of\nthe sequence are real numbers and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a fixed real number; the set of indices starts at one, as in the\nfirst sequence. The quantifier\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∀<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘for all’, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∃<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘there exists’; both were explained in M1-L1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>N may depend on epsilon<\/strong>, but it must not be chosen\nanew for each subsequent n. The order of the quantifiers is crucial. The\ntighter the required accuracy, the further along the sequence the\ncorresponding index threshold may be.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=\\frac1n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand target value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe want to achieve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert a_n-0\\rvert&lt;0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1n&lt;0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe can choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>101<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=101<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfrom the 101st term onwards, <strong>all<\/strong> subsequent values are\nbelow the tolerance of 0.01. At the 100th term, however,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>100<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{100}=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich does not yet satisfy the required <strong>strict<\/strong>\ninequality. In general, for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit suffices to choose a positive integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>greater than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nThen for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe also have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1n&lt;\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nconvergence to zero has thus been shown for <strong>every<\/strong>\npositive tolerance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monotonicity, boundedness and limits<\/h2>\n<p>A sequence is called <strong>monotonically increasing<\/strong> if its\nterms never decrease, and <strong>monotonically decreasing<\/strong> if\nthey never increase. ‘Strictly’ means that the change at each step is\nactually positive or negative, respectively.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_002_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Sequence of points a sub n equals one divided by n for n from one to twelve, positive and strictly decreasing towards zero.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The sequence aₙ = 1/n has positive terms approaching\n0.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=\\frac1n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas positive terms but converges to 0.<\/p>\n<p>The sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n=\\frac1n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis strictly decreasing and bounded below by zero. A fundamental theorem\nof real analysis states: <strong>Every monotone, bounded sequence of\nreal numbers converges.<\/strong> This is an important result concerning\nthe completeness of the real numbers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why can we trust this – idea of the proof?<\/strong> For a\nmonotonically increasing sequence that is bounded above, there is a\nleast upper bound. The terms cannot remain a positive distance away from\nthis bound indefinitely; otherwise, a smaller upper bound would be\npossible. For decreasing sequences, the corresponding argument uses a\ngreatest lower bound. The precise justification relies on a fundamental\nproperty of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncompleteness.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> Monotonicity alone is not sufficient.\nThe sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,2,3,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis strictly increasing but unbounded above; it has no finite real\nlimit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Series: adding infinitely many terms<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>series<\/strong> is defined using the <strong>partial\nsums<\/strong> of a sequence. The nth partial sum contains only the first\nn terms – it is therefore an ordinary finite calculation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_n=a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_n=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘The nth partial sum s sub n is a sub\none plus a sub two and so on up to a sub n. This is the sum of the terms\na sub k for k from one to n.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∑<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘capital sigma’) denotes summation;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the running index and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe upper bound.<\/p>\n<p>An infinite series <strong>converges<\/strong> if the sequence of its\npartial sums has a finite limit.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}a_k=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}s_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The infinite sum of a sub k starting at\nk equal to one is the limit of the partial sums s sub n as n tends to\ninfinity.’ The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∞<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘infinity’ and here indicates the unbounded continuation of\nsummation; we do not simply substitute an infinitely large number for\nit.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: geometric series<\/h3>\n<p>When each term is multiplied by a fixed factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto obtain the next, a geometric sequence arises. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe series converges starting at index zero:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+q+q^2+q^3+\\cdots=\\frac1{1-q}\\qquad\\text{if }\\lvert q\\rvert&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> ‘One plus q plus q squared plus q\ncubed and so on equals one divided by one minus q, provided the absolute\nvalue of q is less than one.’<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example:<\/strong> With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac18+\\cdots=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe partial sums\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.75<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.875<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.875<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproach two from below. This does not mean that any individual summand\never equals two.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Idea of the proof:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q\\ne 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nterms (that is,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+q+\\cdots+q^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ncan be combined into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1-q^{n+1}}{1-q}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe can verify this by multiplying the partial sum by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe middle terms cancel each other out. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q^{n+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero. Therefore,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{1-q}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains in the limit. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, the sum of the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nterms is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand grows without bound. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe partial sums alternate between one and zero. Overall, for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert\\geq 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe infinite geometric series shown does not converge to a finite real\nvalue; for other values with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is likewise no finite real limit of the sums.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Sign countercheck:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=-\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe first partial sums are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac34<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac58<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey fluctuate around the limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{1+\\frac12}=\\frac23<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand nevertheless approach it. <strong>Monotonicity is not necessary for\nconvergence.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Important: terms tending to zero are not enough<\/h2>\n<p>A necessary condition for the convergence of a series is that its\nsummands tend to zero. It is <strong>not sufficient<\/strong>: if the\npartial sums\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconverge to a finite value, then the difference between two consecutive\npartial sums, namely the added summand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmust tend to zero. The converse does not follow.<\/p>\n<p>The so-called <strong>harmonic series<\/strong> is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;1+\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15+\\ldots\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its summands tend to zero, but its partial sums grow without a finite\nupper bound. <strong>Justification by grouping:<\/strong> After the first\nterm, we can form groups\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac12\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac13+\\frac14\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>7<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac15+\\frac16+\\frac17+\\frac18\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand so on. Each group has a value of at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the number of summands doubles each time, while every summand is\nat least as large as the last (smallest) one in its group. An unlimited\nnumber of such groups is added; the partial sums therefore cannot be\nbounded above by a fixed real number.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation of everyday intuition:<\/strong> ‘Each new summand\nbecomes tiny’ does not automatically mean ‘the total sum remains\nfinite’. The rate of decay is crucial.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating with limits – subject to conditions<\/h2>\n<p>When two real sequences converge to finite limits, we can also form\ntheir sums and products in the limit under the usual rules. For a\nquotient, the limit of the denominator must be\n<strong>non-zero<\/strong>, and the quotient must be defined for\nsufficiently late indices.<\/p>\n<p>The situation is different for expressions in which, for example,\n‘zero divided by zero’ appears merely as a limiting form. This is not an\nadmissible division, but a signal that we must examine the limiting\nprocess more closely. Such cases are treated more systematically in\nadvanced analysis lessons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Sequences model iterative methods, for example the repeated\nimprovement of an approximation to an equation. Series occur in filter\ncalculations, number representations and the analysis of processes that\ncan be continued indefinitely. In AI optimisation, convergence concerns\nwhether a method stabilises and under what assumptions a sequence of\nmodel parameters or error values reaches a limit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical limitations:<\/strong> A finite computer does not\nperform infinitely many steps and calculates with limited precision.\nNumerically observing ‘hardly any further change’ is not a proof of\nmathematical convergence; rounding, stagnation or a poorly chosen\nstopping criterion can also create this impression.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The statement ‘monotone and bounded implies convergent’ relies on the\n<strong>completeness of the real numbers<\/strong>; in other number\nsystems, the theorem need not hold in the same form. Only for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert q\\rvert&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay a geometric series be replaced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{1-q}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nif the sum of infinitely many terms is meant. For a\n<strong>finite<\/strong> sum, the appropriate partial-sum formula applies\ninstead.<\/p>\n<p>The epsilon–N definition describes <strong>convergence of a\nreal-valued numerical sequence<\/strong>, not automatically the concept\nof convergence of a sequence of functions or a numerical program. Even\nif all terms are positive, we cannot claim without further justification\nthat the sequence decreases monotonically; the counterexample with a\nnegative geometric factor even exhibits a convergent but non-monotone\nsequence of partial sums. A computer checks only finitely many indices\nwith finite precision; a single small numerical difference is not a\nproof of a limit. Likewise, sums, products and quotients may only be\nreplaced by limits under the respective conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to M1-L3<\/h2>\n<p>With the more precise idea of a limit, we can next give\n<strong>continuity, derivatives and integrals more rigorous\nfoundations<\/strong>. M1-L3 develops the familiar ideas of analysis\nfurther without implicitly assuming more knowledge of symbols.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M1-L3 – University: Analysis II – Continuity, differentiation and integration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · Functions of one\nreal variable<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In upper secondary school, we learnt how to calculate function\nvalues, slopes and integrals. At university, a further question arises:\n<strong>Under what conditions is a calculation justified in the first\nplace?<\/strong> A curve may have a break or a corner at a particular\npoint, or behave so irregularly that familiar methods fail. Mathematical\nanalysis investigates these limiting cases using precise definitions. In\nthis lesson, we initially restrict ourselves to functions that assign\nexactly one real value to each real input.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A function is <strong>continuous<\/strong> if a sufficiently small\nchange in the input allows the change in the result to be made\narbitrarily small, with respect to a fixed point under investigation. It\nis <strong>differentiable<\/strong> if a well-defined local linear\napproximation exists: the graph has a unique finite tangent slope there.\nIn our first notion of integration, <strong>integrable<\/strong> means\nthat a sequence of increasingly fine sums determines a common value.\nThese three properties are related, but they are not identical.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“x nought”<\/td>\n<td>Fixed point at which a function is investigated<\/td>\n<td>Initial value or measurement index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“delta”, lower-case Greek delta<\/td>\n<td>Permitted distance between the input and the point under\ninvestigation<\/td>\n<td>General parameter or variation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon”<\/td>\n<td>Specified positive tolerance for the function value; familiar from\nM1-L2<\/td>\n<td>Error bound in numerical methods<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∀<\/code> / <code>∃<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“for all” / “there exists”<\/td>\n<td>Precise statement about arbitrary tolerances and suitable\nbounds<\/td>\n<td>Logic in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x-x_0\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“absolute value of x minus x nought”<\/td>\n<td>Distance on the number line<\/td>\n<td>Modulus of complex numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lim_{x\\to x_0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“limit as x approaches x nought”<\/td>\n<td>Limit when approaching a point<\/td>\n<td>Limit of a sequence, see M1-L2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“aitch”<\/td>\n<td>Small, non-zero input increment<\/td>\n<td>Height, step size in numerical methods<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“f prime at x nought”<\/td>\n<td>Derivative of f at the point under consideration<\/td>\n<td>A prime may otherwise distinguish a different object<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“capital sigma”<\/td>\n<td>Sum of many individual contributions<\/td>\n<td>Summation symbol in statistics and series<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“delta x”<\/td>\n<td>Width of a subinterval<\/td>\n<td>General change in a value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“integral from a to b”<\/td>\n<td>Accumulation over the interval from a to b<\/td>\n<td>Multidimensional integrals later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“d x”<\/td>\n<td>Variable of integration and formal differential symbol<\/td>\n<td>Different notational context in differential calculus<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\prime}=f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“capital F prime equals lower-case f”<\/td>\n<td>F is an antiderivative of f<\/td>\n<td>Relationship between two functions in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The letters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the <strong>lower and upper limits<\/strong> of the integral.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a function;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere denotes one of its antiderivatives. The square-bracket interval\nnotation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[a,b]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans that both endpoints are included. A colon after a logical\ncondition is read as “such that”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuity: no break at the point under consideration<\/h2>\n<p>The precise question is: can I guarantee <strong>any desired\ntolerance for the result, however small<\/strong>, by choosing a suitably\nsmall tolerance for the input?<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>⟹<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\forall\\varepsilon&gt;0\\ \\exists\\delta&gt;0:\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad |x-x_0|&lt;\\delta\\implies |f(x)-f(x_0)|&lt;\\varepsilon\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> “For every positive epsilon there\nexists a positive delta such that, for all permitted inputs x whose\ndistance from x nought is less than delta, the distance from f of x to f\nof x nought is also less than epsilon.” The condition “for all x”\napplies <strong>only to admissible x in the domain<\/strong>; at boundary\npoints, it may be possible to approach only from the side available\nthere. The point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitself must belong to the domain. The chosen input tolerance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay depend on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand on the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder investigation. Importantly, we are testing continuity <strong>at a\nfixed point<\/strong>, not the stronger property of uniform continuity\nover an entire region. The abbreviated formula must be read as a\nstatement about every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the domain of the function.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – the square function at two.<\/strong> We\nconsider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe relationship between the deviations in input and result is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert f(x)-f(2)\\rvert=\\lvert x^2-4\\rvert=\\lvert x-2\\rvert\\cdot\\lvert x+2\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the input is less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naway from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1&lt;x&lt;3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x+2\\rvert&lt;5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor any desired tolerance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the result, it therefore suffices to choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=\\min(1,\\varepsilon/5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x-2\\rvert&lt;\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x^2-4\\rvert&lt;5\\cdot\\lvert x-2\\rvert&lt;5\\delta\\le\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe final symbol “≤” means “less than or equal to”. The crucial point is\nthe <strong>strict<\/strong> bound in the initial condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x-2\\rvert&lt;\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\neven when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\delta=\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result remains strictly less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans “the smaller of the specified values”. This essentially shows\n<strong>why<\/strong> the square function is continuous at this point,\nrather than merely looking at its graph.<\/p>\n<p>Continuity requires the point under consideration to belong to the\ndomain. The quotient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis undefined at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore, for the natural domain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexcluding zero, “continuity at zero” is <strong>undefined<\/strong>,\nrather than simply “false”. On an extended domain with a specially\nassigned value at zero, the formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncould not be extended continuously to that point, since the values\nbecome unbounded as we approach it. At every other real point,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differentiability: a unique local slope<\/h2>\n<p>In school mathematics M2-L3 (German-language course), the derivative\nwas introduced as the limit of secant slopes. We now consider it as a\nquestion of existence:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x_0)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)}{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> “The derivative of f at x nought is\nthe limit of the quotient of the change in the function value and the\ninput increment h, as h approaches zero.” While division takes place,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be non-zero. At an interior point of the domain, the ordinary\nderivative exists only if the limits from both sides agree and are\nfinite. At an endpoint of an interval, a <strong>one-sided\nderivative<\/strong> may be defined instead; it is not automatically a\ntwo-sided derivative.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – the square function again.<\/strong> At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Limit as <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h}\n&amp;=\\frac{{(2+h)}^2-4}{h}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{4h+h^2}{h}\\\\\n&amp;=4+h\\quad\\text{(for }h\\ne0\\text{)}\\\\\n\\text{Limit as }h\\to0&amp;:\\quad 4\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The slope is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(2)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLocally, the graph can be approximated by a straight line with slope\nfour. The method does not use an “infinitesimal number” as an ordinary\ndivisor, but instead takes a limit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>An important theorem:<\/strong> Differentiability at a point\nimplies continuity there. The converse is not true. The absolute value\nfunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\lvert x\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous at zero, but has a corner: the slope from the left is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand from the right\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince there is no common finite derivative, it is not differentiable\nthere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration: from a collection of rectangles to a limit<\/h2>\n<p>For suitable functions, a definite integral can be understood as a\nlimit of Riemann sums. We divide a finite interval into small\nsubintervals, choose a sample point in each one, multiply its function\nvalue by the width of the subinterval, and add all the products. If\n<strong>every sequence of sufficiently fine partitions<\/strong> in the\nusual Riemann sense gives the same limit, the function is\nintegrable.<\/p>\n<p>For an equal subdivision and right-hand sample points, one possible\nsum is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta x&amp;=\\frac{b-a}{n}\\\\\nS_n&amp;=\\sum_{k=1}^n f(a+k\\cdot\\Delta x)\\cdot\\Delta x\\\\\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&amp;=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}S_n\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Delta x is the length of the interval\ndivided by the number of subintervals. S sub n is the sum of the\nfunction height at each right endpoint multiplied by the subinterval\nwidth. The definite integral is the limit of these sums.” Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of subintervals and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis their running index. The final equation holds for Riemann-integrable\nfunctions; it does not define a number that always exists.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – the straight line on the unit\ninterval.<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach width is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing right-hand sample points, we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">As <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">, <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> approaches <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nS_n&amp;=\\frac{1}{n^2}\\cdot(1+2+\\ldots+n)\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{n+1}{2n}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{As }n\\to\\infty\\text{, }S_n\\text{ approaches }\\tfrac12\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This value equals the area of the right-angled triangle beneath the\nstraight line from zero to one. <strong>Verification for arbitrary\nsample points:<\/strong> the left rectangle sums are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_n=\\frac{n-1}{2n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the right ones are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_n=\\frac{n+1}{2n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis monotonically increasing, every admissible sample-point sum for each\nof these equal partitions lies between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_n-L_n=\\frac1n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero; both limits are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe thus see more than just the convergence of one specially chosen\nright-hand sum. For full Riemann integrability, the corresponding\npartition condition is decisive, not merely a successful sequence of\napproximations. For functions with negative values, however, the\nintegral gives a <strong>signed balance<\/strong>, not automatically a\npositive area (see school mathematics M2-L8, German-language\ncourse).<\/p>\n<p>Every real-valued function that is <strong>continuous on a closed,\nbounded interval<\/strong> is Riemann-integrable there. The converse is\nnot true: a function with finitely many jump discontinuities may also be\nintegrable. Highly discontinuous functions require careful distinctions;\nmeasure theory later introduces a more general notion of\nintegration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The fundamental theorem – why differentiation and\nintegration fit together<\/h2>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[a,b]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an antiderivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere, then:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=F(b)-F(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> “The definite integral of f of x from\na to b is the antiderivative at the upper limit minus the antiderivative\nat the lower limit.” This equation is a statement with prerequisites,\nnot merely a computational trick. <strong>The other direction of the\nfundamental theorem:<\/strong> if, for a function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontinuous on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[a,b]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe define the accumulation function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G^{\\prime}(x)=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat every interior point; suitable one-sided statements apply at the\nendpoints. Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis simply the running variable of integration. For example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=2t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe fundamental theorem thus connects both directions: local\ndifferentiation and accumulation over an interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>numerical methods<\/strong>, continuity explains why small\nchanges in the input cause small changes in the result under suitable\nconditions; this helps in evaluating computational models.\nDifferentiability is important for automatic differentiation,\noptimisation algorithms and local sensitivity analyses. Integrals\ndetermine, for example, the total amount of data or energy accumulated\nfrom a continuous rate. In control engineering and robotics, the clear\ndistinction between an ideal limit and finite measurement resolution is\nparticularly important.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Continuity <strong>at a point<\/strong> does not automatically mean\nuniform continuity throughout the domain. A closed, bounded interval\nprovides further theorems.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Differentiability is stronger than continuity; a corner or cusp may\nprevent a derivative from existing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A definite integral can be negative; “area” and “balance” are not\ninterchangeable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Numerical differences with a very small step size can become\nunstable due to rounding errors. A Riemann sum for finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only an approximation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Riemann notion of integration explained here is not the only\nmathematical notion of integration. Measure theory will be discussed\nlater in greater depth.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>M1-L4<\/strong>, function values no longer depend on just\none input. We will then need partial derivatives, the gradient and\nmultidimensional integrals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are separately listed for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M1-L4 – University: Analysis III – Functions of several variables<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · multivariable\ncalculus<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Many quantities in practice depend on more than one influencing\nvariable. The temperature in a room, for example, varies with position;\nthe running time of a service can depend on load and memory size. A\nsingle slope is not sufficient to describe such relationships. Instead,\nwe ask how a result changes when we vary just one input, and in which\ndirection it increases most rapidly overall. We can also sum values over\nsurfaces and three-dimensional regions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a landscape. Its elevation depends on how far you have\ntravelled east and north. A separate rate of change can be calculated\nfor each of the two coordinates: How steep is the slope towards the east\nwhen the north coordinate is held fixed? How steep is it towards the\nnorth when the east coordinate is held fixed? Together, these two values\nform a vector – the <strong>gradient<\/strong>. In ordinary plane\ngeometry, it points locally in the direction of steepest ascent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read these first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“R two” or “real two-dimensional space”<\/td>\n<td>Pairs of real inputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>A vector space in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>→<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“f maps R two to R”<\/td>\n<td>Function with two real inputs and one real output<\/td>\n<td>The domain may be smaller<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“ordered pair x, y”<\/td>\n<td>Position or pair of inputs<\/td>\n<td>Also a pair of numbers in statistics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∂<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“partial” or “partial d”<\/td>\n<td>Derivative with respect to one input while the other is held\nfixed<\/td>\n<td>Also in partial differential equations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“partial derivative of f with respect to x”<\/td>\n<td>Rate of change in the x-direction only<\/td>\n<td>Distinguished from the ordinary derivative for functions of several\nvariables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“partial derivative of f with respect to y”<\/td>\n<td>Rate of change in the y-direction only<\/td>\n<td>Similarly for other inputs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∇<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“nabla”<\/td>\n<td>Operator that forms the gradient from the partial derivatives<\/td>\n<td>Divergence and curl in vector calculus<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“gradient of f”<\/td>\n<td>Vector of all first partial derivatives<\/td>\n<td>More components for higher-dimensional inputs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“vector u”<\/td>\n<td>Direction, ideally normalised to length one<\/td>\n<td>u can otherwise be a scalar variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_u f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“directional derivative of f in direction u”<\/td>\n<td>Rate of change along u<\/td>\n<td>Other notations are common<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec u\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“norm of vector u”<\/td>\n<td>Vector length<\/td>\n<td>Norms other than the Euclidean norm are possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>∫∫<\/code> /\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“double integral” / “d x d y”<\/td>\n<td>Summation over an area, with a specified region of integration<\/td>\n<td>Differential notation in iterated integrals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Hf<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Hessian matrix of f”<\/td>\n<td>Table of second partial derivatives<\/td>\n<td>H can otherwise denote another operator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Already familiar:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⋅<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cdot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween vectors denotes the dot product (school mathematics M2-L11, in\nGerman);\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis used as a parameter for a path (school mathematics M2-L12, in\nGerman). In what follows,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis read as “f of x and y”. When a pair appears as the argument of a\nfunction, the comma separates the two inputs; it does not denote a\ndecimal number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Partial derivative: only one variable changes<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a model with two inputs:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)=x^2+3xy+y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “f of x and y equals x squared plus\nthree times x times y plus y squared.” If only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges, we treat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas a constant. Similarly, when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges, the variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis held fixed.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&amp;=2x+3y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&amp;=3x+2y\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The partial derivative of f with\nrespect to x is two x plus three y; the partial derivative with respect\nto y is three x plus two y.” This follows from the differentiation rules\nalready encountered, but for only one input at a time.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example.<\/strong> At the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe function value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1,2)=1+6+4=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe partial derivatives there are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>8<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the x-direction and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the y-direction. These are <strong>local rates of change<\/strong>,\nnot two new function values.<\/p>\n<p>It is important to recognise that partial derivatives can exist even\nthough a function as a whole is not continuous or not <strong>totally\ndifferentiable<\/strong> at a point. Total differentiability means that\nthere is a linear approximation whose remainder, relative to the length\nof the change in input, tends to zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Counterexample to a common misconception.<\/strong> Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≠<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)\\neq(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(0,0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong both coordinate axes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis always zero; consequently, both partial derivatives at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexist and are zero. However, if we approach along the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y=t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\neq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t,t)=\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand does not tend to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(0,0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe function is therefore not continuous at the origin and consequently\nis not totally differentiable there. <strong>Two partial derivatives\nalone are not sufficient<\/strong>. Our polynomial\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, is smooth everywhere and satisfies the stronger\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gradient: local change as a vector<\/h2>\n<p>In the Euclidean coordinate system, we write the partial derivatives\nas a vector:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_003_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Local linearised level curves around the point one, two, with the gradient vector eight, seven drawn perpendicular to them.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis perpendicular to the locally linearised level curves.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>At the point (1, 2),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f=(8,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis perpendicular to the linearised level curve.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x},\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The gradient of f is the vector whose\nfirst component is the partial derivative with respect to x and whose\nsecond component is the partial derivative with respect to y.” In our\nexample, this gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f(1,2)=(8,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis totally differentiable, a <strong>non-zero gradient<\/strong> is\nperpendicular to a smooth <strong>level curve<\/strong> – a curve along\nwhich the function has the same value. With ordinary Euclidean\nmeasurement of length, the gradient points in the direction of steepest\nlocal ascent. If the gradient is zero, all unit directions initially\nhave the same directional derivative, zero; no particular direction of\nascent can then be inferred from the gradient. The direction opposite to\na non-zero gradient gives the steepest local descent.<\/p>\n<p>For a <strong>unit vector<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhose norm is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe directional derivative of a differentiable function is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_u f=\\nabla f\\cdot\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud in full:<\/strong> “The directional derivative in\ndirection u is the dot product of the gradient and the unit vector u.”\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}=(\\frac35,\\frac45)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe length is indeed one. Thus the <strong>local directional\nderivative<\/strong> in the example is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>24<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>28<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>52<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8,7)\\cdot(\\frac35,\\frac45)=\\frac{24}{5}+\\frac{28}{5}=\\frac{52}{5}=10.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits of function value per unit of length in this direction. This is\nnot the finite change resulting from a large movement. For comparison,\nthe greatest possible increase per Euclidean unit of length is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>7<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>113<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.630<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\nabla f(1,2)\\rVert=\\sqrt{8^2+7^2}=\\sqrt{113}\\approx10.630<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>10.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis below this value. <code>√<\/code> denotes the square root. Equality\nholds for a unit vector pointing in the direction of the gradient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multivariable chain rule: change along a path<\/h2>\n<p>Sometimes both inputs change simultaneously, for example when a robot\nfollows a trajectory. In that case, we cannot use just the x-derivative\nor just the y-derivative. If we denote the path by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x(t),y(t))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen, assuming differentiability, we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}f(x(t),y(t))=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot x^{\\prime}(t)+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot y^{\\prime}(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The time derivative of the composite\nfunction is the partial derivative with respect to x times the velocity\nin the x-direction, plus the partial derivative with respect to y times\nthe velocity in the y-direction.”\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans the ordinary derivative with respect to the time variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the prime marks <code>′<\/code> denote the corresponding derivatives.\nBoth partial derivatives are evaluated at the current position along the\npath.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example.<\/strong> For the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe path is at the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe gradient there is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\prime}(1)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime}(1)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe <strong>instantaneous rate of change with respect to time<\/strong>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cdot1+5\\cdot2=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe number 15 is not a function value or the total change over a time\ninterval. Direct substitution gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t,t^2)=t^2+3t^3+t^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits ordinary derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2t+9t^2+4t^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich also equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>15<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Double integral: summing over more than a line<\/h2>\n<p>An integral over an area can be used, for example, to sum a density,\na temperature or an elevation function over a region. For a rectangle,\nthe integral can be calculated in one coordinate at a time, provided the\nappropriate conditions are satisfied.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^1(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “First integrate x plus y with respect\nto y from zero to one, then integrate the result with respect to x from\nzero to one; the total value is one.” First,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant and the inner integral gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe outer integral gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12+\\frac12=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For continuous functions on closed rectangles, the order of\nintegration may be reversed accordingly (an appropriate special case of\nFubini’s theorem). In our example, the reverse order also gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^1(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfirst we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12+y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand then again\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor non-rectangular regions, the limits often depend on the other\nvariable; when reversing the order, these limits must be rewritten\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Second derivatives and local curvature – outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>Hessian matrix<\/strong> collects the second partial\nderivatives of a sufficiently smooth function. It helps to assess\ncurvature in different directions and thus, for example, to classify\nstationary points in optimisation problems.<\/p>\n<p>For our example function:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>H<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nHf&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}2&amp;3\\\\3&amp;2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The entries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresult from differentiating twice with respect to the same coordinate;\nthe two entries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the mixed derivatives. If the second partial derivatives are\ncontinuous in a neighbourhood, the two mixed derivatives agree\n(Schwarz’s theorem); the Hessian matrix is then symmetric. This\ncondition is satisfied for the polynomial. At the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe gradient is zero, yet there is <strong>no minimum<\/strong>: along\nthe direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y=t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe function value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive, whereas along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=t,\\ y=-t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis negative. Such a point is called a <strong>saddle point<\/strong>.\nHere the determinant of the Hessian matrix also indicates mixed\ncurvature:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot2-3\\cdot3=-5&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe matrix therefore has eigenvalues of opposite signs; the stationary\npoint is a strict saddle point. This second-derivative test requires a\nsufficiently smooth function and applies to an <strong>interior\nstationary point<\/strong>. Once again, we see that a vanishing first\nderivative alone does not establish whether there is an extremum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In neural networks, every model error depends on many parameters; the\ngradient assigns a local rate of change to each parameter, making\ngradient-based methods possible. In robotics and computer graphics,\npartial derivatives describe changes in surfaces, trajectories and\ncoordinate transformations. Double integrals give, for example, the\ntotal mass of a surface from its surface density, or the probability of\na two-dimensional region given a joint density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A partial derivative initially considers only one coordinate\ndirection; its existence alone guarantees neither continuity nor total\ndifferentiability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Interpreting the gradient as the direction of steepest ascent\nrequires a specified geometry or norm; the Euclidean one is\ncustomary.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Expressing a directional derivative as a dot product requires\ndifferentiability. Without a normalised direction vector, the rate of\nchange is scaled differently.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Integration over a region requires limits and – depending on the\ntheorem – suitable conditions such as continuity or integrability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In numerical optimisation, the gradient can be zero even though no\nminimum has been found.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>The rates of change of several variables are vectors; their\ncomposition is described using matrices. <strong>M1-L5<\/strong>\ntherefore first clarifies what vector spaces, bases and linear maps\nactually are.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M1-L5 – University: Linear algebra I – Vector spaces, bases and mappings<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · Linear\nalgebra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>At school, vectors were mostly arrows in two- or three-dimensional\nspace. University-level linear algebra generalises this idea: lists of\ndata, polynomials and functions can also form vectors if their elements\ncan be added and scaled by numbers in an appropriate way. What matters\nare the <strong>rules of calculation<\/strong>, not the image of a\ngeometric arrow. A basis describes the simplest building blocks from\nwhich every vector in a space can be constructed uniquely. A linear map\ntransforms these building blocks according to rules that are compatible\nwith addition and scaling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A file containing two measurements can be described as a pair. You\ncan add two such pairs of data component by component and scale all the\nvalues by the same factor. With two suitably chosen basic directions,\nevery pair can be represented. What works in the plane can also apply to\nlong lists of parameters of an AI model and even to polynomials.\nMatrices record how a linear transformation changes the chosen basic\ndirections.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_004_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A unit square is transformed into a parallelogram under the linear map with matrix \\[\\[2,1\\],\\[0,1\\]\\].\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The linear map A(x, y) = (2x + y, y) transforms a\nsquare into a parallelogram.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Linear map A(x,y) = (2x + y, y): a square is sheared and\nstretched.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vee’<\/td>\n<td>General vector space<\/td>\n<td>Volume in the German school course M1-L5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘R to the power n’<\/td>\n<td>Vectors with n real components<\/td>\n<td>Special case of a vector space<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘vector v’, ‘vector w’<\/td>\n<td>Elements of a vector space<\/td>\n<td>Also written without arrows in abstract spaces<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘zero vector’<\/td>\n<td>Identity element of vector addition<\/td>\n<td>Not to be confused with the single real number zero<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘lambda’<\/td>\n<td>Real number by which a vector is scaled<\/td>\n<td>Often an eigenvalue later in M1-L6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">span<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{span}(\\vec u,\\vec v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘span of u and v’<\/td>\n<td>Set of all linear combinations of the specified vectors<\/td>\n<td>Technical term: <em>linear span<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘alpha’, ‘beta’<\/td>\n<td>Coefficients of a linear combination<\/td>\n<td>Often also angles or statistical parameters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dim(V)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dimension of V’<\/td>\n<td>Number of vectors in a basis of a finite-dimensional space<\/td>\n<td>Also the dimension of geometric objects<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec e_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec e_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘e one’, ‘e two’<\/td>\n<td>Standard basis vectors<\/td>\n<td>Other spaces use other bases<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T:V\\to W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘T maps V to W’<\/td>\n<td>Transformation between vector spaces<\/td>\n<td>T can otherwise denote time or another operator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix A’<\/td>\n<td>Table of numbers representing a linear map in chosen bases<\/td>\n<td>A also denotes an area or an event elsewhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix A times vector x’<\/td>\n<td>Matrix–vector product<\/td>\n<td>Not ordinary multiplication of numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ker(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘kernel of T’<\/td>\n<td>All inputs mapped to the zero vector<\/td>\n<td>In software, kernel has a different meaning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘P two’<\/td>\n<td>Vector space of real polynomials of degree at most two<\/td>\n<td>Other notations depending on the textbook<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a <strong>positive integer<\/strong> and gives the number of\ncomponents.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a possible target vector space. The word <strong>scalar<\/strong>\nhere means an ordinary real number that multiplies a vector. Other\nvector spaces can be defined over other number systems; for now, we stay\nwith real scalars.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is a vector space?<\/h2>\n<p>A real vector space consists of a set of objects and two operations:\n<strong>vector addition<\/strong> and <strong>multiplication of a vector\nby a real number<\/strong>. In particular, addition must be associative\nand commutative, there must be a zero vector and additive inverses, and\nthe operations must satisfy the distributive laws. In addition,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\lambda\\mu)\\cdot\\vec v=\\lambda\\cdot(\\mu\\cdot\\vec v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhold for real numbers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda,\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere denote ordinary real scalars;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘mu’) is a second scalar. Furthermore, addition and scaling must not\nproduce elements outside the space (<strong>closure<\/strong>). These\nrules are chosen so that familiar algebraic manipulations can still be\ncarried out.<\/p>\n<p>The simplest example is the plane\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose vectors are ordered pairs of real numbers:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n(2,3)+(1,-4)&amp;=(3,-1)\\\\\n2(2,3)&amp;=(4,6)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The vector two, three plus the vector\none, minus four gives the vector three, minus one. Two times the vector\ntwo, three gives four, six.’ Addition and scaling are performed\ncomponent by component.<\/p>\n<p>Another surprising example is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe set of all real polynomials of the form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+bx+cx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf you add two such polynomials or multiply one by a real number, the\nresult is again a polynomial of degree at most two. A polynomial is\ntherefore a <strong>vector<\/strong> here, even though it does not look\nlike an ordinary arrow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linear combinations, independence and bases<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>linear combination<\/strong> is formed when we multiply\nseveral vectors by individual factors and then add them together.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{w}=\\alpha\\vec{u}+\\beta\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Vector w equals alpha times vector u\nplus beta times vector v.’ The set of all vectors that can be obtained\nin this way is called the <strong>space spanned<\/strong> by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndenoted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">span<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{span}(\\vec u,\\vec v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Vectors are <strong>linearly independent<\/strong> if none of them can\nbe expressed non-trivially as a combination of the others. More\nprecisely, their linear combination can equal the zero vector only if\nall the factors are zero. A <strong>basis<\/strong> is a selection of\nvectors that is both linearly independent and spans the entire vector\nspace. The representation of any vector in such a basis is unique.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – an unusual basis of the plane.<\/strong> We\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec u=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v=(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCan we represent the vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec w=(4,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nThe conditions for the components are:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>α<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>α<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha+\\beta&amp;=4\\\\\n\\alpha-\\beta&amp;=2\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Adding the two equations gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\alpha=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe result can be checked directly:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot(1,1)+1\\cdot(1,-1)=(3,3)+(1,-1)=(4,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The two original vectors do indeed form a basis of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause neither is a multiple of the other. <strong>Proof of\nuniqueness:<\/strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\cdot(1,1)+\\beta\\cdot(1,-1)=(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha+\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha-\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhold. It follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nestablishing linear independence. Conversely, for every pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p,q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain the solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\frac{p+q}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\frac{p-q}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe whole plane is therefore spanned. The corresponding matrix with\nthese vectors as its columns has determinant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is invertible. The <strong>dimension<\/strong> of this space is two:\nevery basis has exactly two vectors. In contrast, in the standard basis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec e_1=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec e_2=(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe same vector would have coordinates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Coordinates depend on the basis; the abstract vector does\nnot.<\/strong> In our example, the coordinates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the new basis correspond to the standard coordinate pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout specifying the basis, the same list of numbers can denote\ndifferent vectors.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example, the set of three polynomials\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a basis. Its dimension is three, because each expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+bx+cx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis uniquely described by exactly three coefficients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linear maps: compatible with addition and scaling<\/h2>\n<p>A map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween real vector spaces is <strong>linear<\/strong> if it maps sums to\nsums and scaled vectors to correspondingly scaled results:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\vec{u}+\\vec{v})&amp;=T(\\vec{u})+T(\\vec{v})\\\\\nT(\\lambda\\vec{u})&amp;=\\lambda T(\\vec{u})\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘T of u plus v equals T of u plus T of\nv; T of lambda times u equals lambda times T of u.’ In particular, it\nfollows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(\\vec0)=\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(\\vec0)=T(0\\cdot\\vec v)=0\\cdot T(\\vec v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe image of the zero vector must be the zero vector. For\nfinite-dimensional spaces, it is also sufficient to know the images of\nall the basis vectors: they determine the linear map uniquely.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example.<\/strong> The rule\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x,y)=(2x+y,x-y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis linear. The input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe underlying matrix in the standard basis is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>first column<\/strong> shows the image of the first\nstandard basis vector:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(1,0)=(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>second column<\/strong> shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(0,1)=(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe matrix can therefore transform any other input by combining its two\ncolumns:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot(3,1)=3\\cdot(2,1)+1\\cdot(1,-1)=(7,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Matrix A times vector three, one equals\nthree times the first column plus one times the second column, giving\nseven, two.’ The table of numbers describes the linear map\n<strong>relative to chosen bases<\/strong>. If we change the basis, its\nmatrix representation changes, not the underlying map.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Kernel and image – what is lost, what can be reached?<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>kernel<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ker(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains precisely the inputs that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaps to the zero vector. The <strong>image<\/strong> of the map contains\nall outputs that can be reached. If the kernel consists only of the zero\nvector, the map is injective: different inputs cannot produce the same\noutput.<\/p>\n<p>For the matrix just introduced, the determinant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot(-1)-1\\cdot1=-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand is therefore non-zero. Consequently, this map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a <strong>linear inverse map<\/strong>, and its kernel consists only\nof the zero vector. Its output can reach every vector in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIndeed, for a desired output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p,q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe equations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2x+y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=x-y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\frac{p+q}{3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\frac{p-2q}{3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe inverse calculation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Counterexample:<\/strong> The map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(x,y)=(x,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich is also linear, has kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{(0,y)\\mid y\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand image only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{(t,t)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is neither injective nor surjective onto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA kernel consisting only of the zero vector indicates\n<strong>injectivity<\/strong> for linear maps; for rectangular matrices,\nhowever, surjectivity need not follow. A non-zero determinant provides\nthe corresponding criterion for invertibility only for square matrices\nbetween spaces of equal finite dimension. <strong>M1-L6<\/strong>\ndevelops these ideas further through rank, determinants and\neigenvalues.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>In computer graphics, rotations, reflections and scaling about the\norigin are linear maps; their matrices allow compact calculations that\ncan be combined in sequence. An AI embedding is a list of coordinates in\na high-dimensional vector space; matrices transform such representations\ninto new feature spaces. In numerical equation solvers, large linear\nsystems and the question of their solvability are analysed using rank\nand kernel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Not every transformation is linear. The translation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>↦<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)\\mapsto(x+1,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>affine<\/strong>: it does not map the zero vector to the zero\nvector.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The <strong>choice of basis<\/strong> affects the coordinate\nrepresentation. The same geometric or abstract vector can therefore have\ndifferent lists of numbers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mathematical vector spaces do not have to be finite-dimensional. The\nexamples discussed here are finite-dimensional.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Real computers often store only approximations of the matrix\nentries. Numerical instability also depends on how strongly a\ntransformation amplifies small errors. The mathematical existence of an\ninverse matrix does not guarantee that its numerical computation is\nwell-conditioned.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>M1-L6<\/strong>, we investigate how many independent\ndirections a matrix preserves, when it is invertible and which\ndirections are merely scaled by the transformation. This leads to\n<strong>rank, determinants, eigenvalues and eigenvectors<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M1-L6 – University: Linear algebra II – Rank, determinants and eigenvalues<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · linear algebra\nII<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In M1-L5 we learnt that a matrix describes a linear transformation in\nchosen bases. We now investigate this transformation in more detail.\n<strong>Rank<\/strong> tells us how many independent output directions\nare preserved. The <strong>determinant<\/strong> of a square matrix helps\nus identify whether a transformation collapses an area or volume to\nzero. <strong>Eigenvectors<\/strong> are special directions that remain\non the same line under the transformation; the corresponding\n<strong>eigenvalue<\/strong> tells us how they are scaled or reversed.\nThese properties help us understand large models without having to try\nevery individual input separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a sheet of squared paper being stretched, reflected or\ncompressed. After ordinary stretching, a square becomes a parallelogram:\nthe sheet still has two independent directions. If the entire sheet\ncollapses onto a single line, a dimension is lost. There are often\ndirections that remain on the same straight line during deformation:\nthese are eigendirections. In higher-dimensional datasets, this\ncorresponds to loss of information or particular amplification of\ncertain combinations of features.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the formulas<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in this lesson<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“matrix A”<\/td>\n<td>Table of numbers representing the linear transformation<\/td>\n<td>Area, event or set in other chapters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">rang<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{rang}(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">rank<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{rank}(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“rank of A”<\/td>\n<td>Number of linearly independent columns or rows<\/td>\n<td><code>rank<\/code> in code may refer to a ranking<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“determinant of A”<\/td>\n<td>Single number associated with a <strong>square<\/strong> matrix<\/td>\n<td>Also\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert A\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin some books; beware of absolute value/cardinality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“lambda”<\/td>\n<td>Eigenvalue; a number, possibly complex<\/td>\n<td>Parameter or scaling factor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“vector v”<\/td>\n<td>Non-zero eigenvector<\/td>\n<td>General vector from M1-L5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“A times vector v”<\/td>\n<td>Matrix-vector product<\/td>\n<td>Not the multiplication of two ordinary numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“identity matrix I”<\/td>\n<td>Matrix that leaves vectors unchanged<\/td>\n<td>The letter I may also be an index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“zero vector”<\/td>\n<td>Vector whose components are all zero<\/td>\n<td>Not the same kind of object as the number 0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“not equal”<\/td>\n<td>Inequality,\ne.g. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec v\\ne\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>General comparison symbol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“A to the power of minus one”, inverse matrix<\/td>\n<td>Inverse transformation for an invertible matrix<\/td>\n<td>For numbers, the power minus one means reciprocal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(\\ldots)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“diagonal matrix with ...”<\/td>\n<td>Numbers only on the main diagonal<\/td>\n<td>Also a utility function in programs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>⊥<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“perpendicular to”, orthogonal<\/td>\n<td>Dot product zero in ordinary Euclidean space<\/td>\n<td>Geometric perpendicularity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ker(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“kernel of A”<\/td>\n<td>Input vectors that A maps to the zero vector<\/td>\n<td>Kernel of general linear transformations as well<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dim<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dim<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“dimension”<\/td>\n<td>Number of basis vectors of a space<\/td>\n<td>In computer science may refer to the shape of an array<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“matrix P”, “diagonal matrix D”<\/td>\n<td>Eigenvector columns and corresponding eigenvalues<\/td>\n<td>P denotes probability in M1-L7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The letters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the numbers of rows and columns of a matrix, respectively. An\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmatrix has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns. The identity matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same square dimensions as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen both are combined in a formula. Eigenvalues may be complex even for\nreal matrices; we begin with examples that have real eigenvalues.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rank: how many independent directions remain?<\/h2>\n<p>The columns of a matrix describe the images of the standard basis\nvectors. The <strong>rank<\/strong> is the dimension of the space spanned\nby these columns. It is also the maximum number of linearly independent\nrows. For a matrix with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns, the rank cannot exceed the smaller of these numbers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example: a projection onto a line.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nB&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\2&amp;4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second column\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis twice the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere is therefore only <strong>one<\/strong> independent column\ndirection:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">rank<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{rank}(B)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe transformation can reach only points on a straight line in the\ntarget space. Some input information is lost in the process. For\nexample,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\cdot(-2,1)=(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\ntwo inputs that differ by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the same image. The kernel therefore contains more than just the\nzero vector. For a linear transformation from an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-dimensional\nspace, the <strong>rank-nullity theorem<\/strong> states:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ker<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">rank<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dim\\ker(A)+\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+1=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>By comparison, the identity matrix of the plane has two independent\ncolumns; its rank is two. Rank also provides the basis for determining\nwhether systems of linear equations are solvable: if the coefficient\nmatrix and the matrix augmented by the right-hand side have the same\nrank, the system is solvable. For a unique solution, this rank must\nadditionally equal the number of unknowns. These conditions apply to\nlinear systems over a field, here the real numbers.\n<strong>Countercheck:<\/strong> for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\cdot(x,y)=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe augmented matrix has rank two, but the coefficient matrix has rank\nonly one: the system has no solution. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\cdot(x,y)=(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nboth ranks equal one; there are infinitely many solutions because there\nare two unknowns and only one independent equation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Determinant: is area or volume collapsed?<\/h2>\n<p>In the usual sense, the determinant is defined only for\n<strong>square matrices<\/strong>. For a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmatrix with entries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the first row and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c,d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the second row, the calculation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>a<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>d<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{bmatrix}\\\\\n\\det(A)&amp;=a\\cdot d-b\\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The determinant of A is a times d minus\nb times c.” Each letter here names the entry at a fixed position in the\nmatrix. The absolute value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert\\det(A)\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the <strong>area scaling factor<\/strong> of a linear\ntransformation in the plane, or the corresponding volume scaling factor\nin three dimensions. A negative sign indicates reversal of\norientation.<\/p>\n<p>For the matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshown above,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(B)=1\\cdot4-2\\cdot2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA square can therefore shrink to a line segment with zero area. It\nfollows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not invertible. <strong>For square matrices:<\/strong> a non-zero\ndeterminant, full rank and the existence of an inverse matrix are\nequivalent properties.<\/p>\n<p>A large or small determinant alone, however, does\n<strong>not<\/strong> tell us whether a numerical calculation is stable:\nvery different scaling factors can cancel each other out in the\ndeterminant. We examine numerical <em>conditioning<\/em> in M1-L8.\nExample:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1000000<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"><\/mspace><mn>0.000001<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(1000000,\\;0.000001)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas determinant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut a condition number of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the Euclidean norm. An area scaling factor of one therefore does not\nrule out an extremely sensitive direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Eigenvectors: directions that remain directions<\/h2>\n<p>An eigenvector is a non-zero vector whose image under a square matrix\nlies on the same straight line. The eigenvalue is the corresponding\nscaling factor.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_005_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Matrix A with diagonal entries two and one: e1 is scaled to 2e1, e2 remains e2.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Eigenvectors: the first coordinate direction is\nscaled by 2; the second remains unchanged.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Eigenvectors retain their direction; in this example the eigenvalues\nare 2 and 1.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≠<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\vec{v}=\\lambda\\vec{v}\\qquad\\text{with }\\vec{v}\\neq\\vec{0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Matrix A times vector v equals lambda\ntimes vector v, where v is not the zero vector.” The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be positive, negative or zero. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe eigenvector is mapped to the zero vector; this is possible for a\nsingular matrix. Its original line is an eigenspace, even though the\nzero vector itself has no direction. The term\n<strong>eigendirection<\/strong>, when the image is non-zero, means the\nline determined by a real eigenvector.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked mini-example – two particularly simple\ndirections.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2\\end{bmatrix}\\\\\nA\\cdot(1,1)&amp;=(3,3)=3\\cdot(1,1)\\\\\nA\\cdot(1,-1)&amp;=(1,-1)=1\\cdot(1,-1)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis scaled by a factor of three; its eigenvalue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains unchanged; its eigenvalue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe have thus found the two real eigenvalues and corresponding\neigenvectors. The rank of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis two and the determinant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot2-1\\cdot1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis agrees with the product of the two eigenvalues\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How do we find eigenvalues systematically?<\/h2>\n<p>The eigenvalue equation can be rearranged:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec v-\\lambda\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A-\\lambda I)\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a square matrix, a non-zero solution is possible if and only if the\ncorresponding determinant is zero.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The determinant of A minus lambda times\nthe identity matrix equals zero.” This is called the\n<strong>characteristic equation<\/strong>. In our example it becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{(2-\\lambda)}^2-1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+3=(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda-3)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsolving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A-3I)\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=-v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn general, the system of linear equations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mn>0<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A-\\lambda I)\\cdot\\vec v=\\vec0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be solved for each eigenvalue. Each eigenvector can be scaled by\nany <strong>non-zero<\/strong> number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagonalisation and limitations of the method<\/h2>\n<p>If an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\times n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmatrix has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlinearly independent eigenvectors, these can be assembled into a matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the diagonal matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof corresponding eigenvalues we have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=P\\cdot D\\cdot P^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “A equals P times D times the inverse of\nmatrix P.” This representation simplifies repeated matrix\nmultiplication, because powers of a diagonal matrix are calculated\ncomponent by component. <strong>Not every matrix is\ndiagonalisable.<\/strong> Counterexample:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas only the eigenvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(counted twice), but\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(J-I)\\cdot(x,y)=(y,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere is therefore only <strong>one<\/strong> independent eigenvector\ndirection; an invertible eigenvector matrix P with two columns is\nimpossible. A real symmetric matrix such as our example, by contrast,\nhas an orthonormal basis of real eigenvectors, that is, pairwise\nperpendicular unit vectors, and is particularly easy to work with.\nNon-symmetric matrices may have complex eigenvalues or incomplete\nsystems of eigenvectors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>PCA and data analysis:<\/strong> Eigenvectors of a suitably\ncalculated covariance matrix describe directions of variation; the\neigenvalues quantify the corresponding variance. This requires\nappropriate data preprocessing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Dynamical systems and robotics:<\/strong> Eigenvalues of\nsystem matrices provide information about stability and the behaviour of\nrepeated state transitions; for discrete linear systems, their absolute\nvalues are particularly relevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Image processing and AI:<\/strong> Matrix rank, compression\nand low-dimensional approximations help identify redundant data\ndirections. In practice, singular value decomposition (SVD) is often\nused instead of direct eigendecomposition.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common misinterpretations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An <strong>eigenvector must not be the zero vector<\/strong>;\notherwise the equation would be trivially satisfied for any lambda.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An <strong>eigenvalue of zero<\/strong>, by contrast, is explicitly\npossible and indicates a loss of information for square matrices.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Only square matrices have eigenvalues in the sense used here; for\nrectangular matrices, <strong>singular values<\/strong> are often the\nmore appropriate tool.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The determinant is not a general measure of computational accuracy.\nIn particular, an invertible matrix can be numerically very poorly\nconditioned.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mathematical linear independence is defined exactly. With rounded\ndata, a numerical algorithm often determines an <em>effective<\/em> rank\nonly on the basis of a tolerance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>M1-L7 moves from deterministic transformations to uncertain\nquantities: probability distributions, expected value, conditional\ninformation and statistical inference. Later we combine the two, for\nexample in the covariance matrix and in AI models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M1-L7 – University: Probability theory and mathematical statistics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core area of university study · Probability\ntheory and statistics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematical model often describes a clear relationship: an input\nproduces a specified output. With weather, sensors, network latencies or\nAI classifications, however, the outcome is uncertain. Probability\ntheory describes such uncertainty through <strong>events<\/strong>,\n<strong>random variables<\/strong> and <strong>distributions<\/strong>.\nStatistics reverses the direction: from finitely many observed data\npoints, we attempt to estimate properties of an underlying model. The\nessential distinction is therefore: <strong>probability proceeds from\nmodel assumptions to possible data; statistics draws conclusions about\nmodel parameters from data.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a program takes different amounts of time to process a file.\nInstead of predicting one exact duration, we describe possible running\ntimes and their probabilities. An expected value summarises the\nlong-term average running time, while the variance describes\nfluctuations. After many actual measurements, we can estimate these\nunknown quantities – but must take account of the remaining uncertainty.\nA statistic therefore gives no certainty about an individual future\nprogram run.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>capital omega<\/td>\n<td>Set of all possible elementary outcomes<\/td>\n<td>In physics, also ohms or a spatial domain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>event A, event B<\/td>\n<td>Subsets of possible outcomes<\/td>\n<td>Also matrices in linear algebra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“P of A”<\/td>\n<td>Probability of event A<\/td>\n<td>P often denotes a point or polynomial space<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cap B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“A intersection B”<\/td>\n<td>Both events occur<\/td>\n<td>Intersection of sets in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“P of A given B”<\/td>\n<td>Probability of A given that B is known<\/td>\n<td><code>\\|<\/code> also denotes absolute value or divisibility<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“capital X”<\/td>\n<td>Random variable: a number is assigned to each outcome<\/td>\n<td>In M1-L5, a vector or input variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“P of X equals x”<\/td>\n<td>Probability of a discrete value<\/td>\n<td>Usually zero for a single value in continuous distributions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“expected value of X”<\/td>\n<td>Weighted theoretical mean<\/td>\n<td>E also denotes the number of edges in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“variance of X”<\/td>\n<td>Mean squared deviation from the expected value<\/td>\n<td><code>var<\/code> often declares a variable in code<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>lowercase sigma<\/td>\n<td>Standard deviation in the model<\/td>\n<td>In physics, e.g. stress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code>, <code>∑<\/code><\/td>\n<td>capital sigma / summation sign<\/td>\n<td>Addition over several possible values<\/td>\n<td>General summation notation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“mu”<\/td>\n<td>Expected value of the population under consideration<\/td>\n<td>Often also a mean in physics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“en”<\/td>\n<td>Number of observed measurements<\/td>\n<td>Dimension, exponent, index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“X bar”<\/td>\n<td>Mean of a particular sample<\/td>\n<td>Not a theoretical expected value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_X(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“F sub X of t”<\/td>\n<td>Distribution function: probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\le t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>F otherwise denotes a function or force<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_X(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“f sub X of t”<\/td>\n<td>Density of a continuous random variable<\/td>\n<td>Not a pointwise probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“H nought”<\/td>\n<td>Null hypothesis of a test<\/td>\n<td>H denotes other quantities in other fields<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“p-value” (when explicitly so named)<\/td>\n<td>Data-based measure of departure from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noften denotes a probability of success<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CI<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“confidence interval”<\/td>\n<td>Interval calculated from data using a specified coverage rule<\/td>\n<td>Abbreviation for <em>confidence interval<\/em><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“alpha”<\/td>\n<td>Preselected significance level of a hypothesis test<\/td>\n<td>Also an angle in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi>σ<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“sigma divided by the square root of n”<\/td>\n<td>Standard error of the sample mean with independent observations of\nequal variance<\/td>\n<td>Not generally valid for dependent samples<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1{,}96<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“one point nine six”<\/td>\n<td>Approximate factor for two-sided 95% normal intervals<\/td>\n<td>Not a universal factor for every distribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The capital letters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare placeholders; only their role in the respective section is fixed.\nThe sign <code>∑<\/code> was already used in School Mathematics M2-L16,\nbut is applied here to general discrete distributions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probability space: Which outcomes are possible?<\/h2>\n<p>An elementary experiment has a set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof all possible outcomes. An <strong>event<\/strong> is a subset of this\nset. A probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassigns a number between zero and one to each admissible event. The\ncertain event has probability one; the probabilities of pairwise\ndisjoint events can be added. More precisely, <strong>countable\nadditivity<\/strong> applies: for a finite or countably infinite family\nof <strong>pairwise disjoint<\/strong> admissible events, the probability\nof their union is the sum of their probabilities. In the full\nuniversity-level theory, admissible events are specified by a\n<strong>sigma-algebra<\/strong>; it contains the underlying set and is\nclosed under complements and countable unions. For finite examples, the\npower set of all outcomes is sufficient to begin with.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example – fair die:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEvent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\{2,4,6\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes an even number of spots, and event\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\{4,5,6\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na number greater than three. We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=\\frac36=\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe joint case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cap B=\\{4,6\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac26=\\frac13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese values assume <strong>a fair die<\/strong>; a biased die has\ndifferent weights.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditional probability and independence<\/h2>\n<p>When event B is already known, we consider only the cases in which B\noccurs. This is the idea of conditional probability:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">provided that <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\\qquad\\text{provided that }P(B)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The probability of A given B is the\nprobability of A and B jointly, divided by the probability of B.” Here\nthe vertical bar means “given”, not division. In the die example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{\\frac13}{\\frac12}=\\frac23<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis quotient is <strong>not<\/strong> defined; elementary conditional\nprobability must not be calculated in this way. More general conditional\ndistributions are an advanced concept.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Independence<\/strong> is stronger than merely having a small\ncorrelation: two events are independent if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)\\cdot P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf B has positive probability, it follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not true of the events in the die example, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Bayes’ formula as a change of perspective:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(B\\mid A)\\cdot P(A)}{P(B)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “A given B equals B given A times A,\ndivided by B.” The formula rearranges the same joint probability. In AI\nclassification it is helpful when the probability of an alarm\n<strong>given a fault<\/strong> is known, but we want to know how likely\na <strong>fault is given an alarm<\/strong>. The base rate of faults is\nalso required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical example:<\/strong> Only 1% of all files contain an\nerror. A checker detects 90% of erroneous files and falsely raises an\nalarm for 5% of intact files. Among 10,000 files, we expect about 90\ntrue alarms (out of 100 erroneous files) and 495 false alarms (out of\n9,900 intact files). Thus, of 585 alarms, only 90 correspond to actual\nerrors:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>90<\/mn><mn>585<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{90}{585}\\approx15{,}4\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the exact value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>90<\/mn><mn>585<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{90}{585}=\\frac{2}{13}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThese are <strong>modelled expected frequencies<\/strong>; the observed\ncounts for 10,000 actual random files need not be exactly the same. A\nhigh detection rate for errors does not automatically mean high\nreliability <em>of a positive alarm<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random variable, distribution and expected value<\/h2>\n<p>A random variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassigns a numerical value to each elementary outcome. In a\n<strong>general probability space<\/strong>, it must also be\n<em>measurable<\/em>: for every real threshold, the event\n“<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis at or below this threshold” belongs to the admissible sigma-algebra.\nOtherwise its probability would not necessarily be defined. In our\nfinite examples with the power set as the system of events, this\ncondition is automatically satisfied. The <strong>distribution<\/strong>\nspecifies the probabilities with which the values occur. For discrete\nvariables, the probabilities of individual values can be listed.<\/p>\n<p>For discrete values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith probabilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=\\sum_i x_i\\cdot p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The expected value of X is the sum over\nall values i of x sub i times p sub i.” The subscript\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a running index;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstands for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare non-negative and sum to one. With infinitely many possible values,\nthe expected value is guaranteed to be defined as a finite number only\nunder suitable summability conditions; for example, it suffices that the\nsum of the absolute values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert x_i\\rvert\\cdot p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis finite.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:<\/strong> A workload takes the values 0, 1 or\n2 with probabilities 0.2, 0.5 and 0.3. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[X]=0\\cdot0{,}2+1\\cdot0{,}5+2\\cdot0{,}3=1{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not mean that 1.1 tasks can occur in one trial, but describes\nthe long-term mean under the model.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>variance<\/strong> measures the mean squared deviation\nfrom the expected value:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[{(X-E[X])}^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The variance of X is the expected value\nof the square of the deviation of X from its own expected value.” In the\nexample,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[X^2]=0^2\\cdot0{,}2+1^2\\cdot0{,}5+2^2\\cdot0{,}3=1{,}7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>49<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1{,}7-{1{,}1}^2=0{,}49<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation is its square root:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0{,}7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntasks. It has the same unit as the original random variable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous distributions: density is not probability<\/h2>\n<p>For a random variable <strong>that can be described by a\ndensity<\/strong>, we consider a <strong>density<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead of individual point probabilities. Not every distribution is\neither purely discrete or has a density: mixtures and singular\ndistributions are also possible. The probability between two bounds is\nobtained by <strong>integrating<\/strong> the density; the distribution\nfunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_X(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the probability of obtaining at most the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a\\leq X\\leq b)=\\int_a^b f_X(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The probability that X lies between a\nand b is the integral of the density f sub X from a to b with respect to\nt.” The variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis merely the variable of integration here. For the formula to hold,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be non-negative and integrate to one over the entire real line; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\le b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe endpoints then make no difference. For a distribution described by\nan ordinary density,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=a)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neven if the density is high at that point. The normal distribution is a\ncommon example of a continuous distribution, but by no means every data\nphenomenon is normally distributed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Statistics: drawing conclusions about unknown properties\nfrom data<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>sample mean<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the sum of the observed values divided by their number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is an estimate of the unknown theoretical expected value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the data collection process is suitable. For independent and\nidentically distributed observations with a finite expected value, the\nlaw of large numbers describes the long-term convergence of the sample\nmean towards\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that every finite sample must be\nclose to the true mean. With finite variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sample mean has variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand standard error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi>σ<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin the presence of dependencies this formula is generally not\ncorrect.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>confidence interval<\/strong> is calculated from random data\naccording to a specified rule. Under the model assumptions of a 95%\nmethod, about 95% of these intervals should cover the fixed unknown\nparameter over very many repetitions. This is <strong>not<\/strong> a\nstatement that, after observing a particular frequentist interval, the\nalready fixed parameter lies within it with probability 95%. Unreliable\nor biased samples do not automatically become correct just because the\ninterval is narrow.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked confidence interval:<\/strong> Suppose we have 100\nindependent normally distributed measurements, the population standard\ndeviation is <strong>known<\/strong> to be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the observed mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen the standard error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>100<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{100}}=0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the normal model the two-sided 95% interval is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\pm1{,}96\\cdot0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>608<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>392<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[9{,}608;\\,10{,}392]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nIf the standard deviation is unknown or other assumptions apply, this\nsimple normal formula cannot be used without further justification.<\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>hypothesis test<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a null hypothesis. Under that null model, a p-value is the\nprobability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the observed\none according to the chosen test statistic criterion. It is\n<strong>not<\/strong> the probability that the null hypothesis is true,\nnor a measure of the practical magnitude of an effect. Study design,\nmultiple testing, assumptions and the selection of measurements are\ncrucial. A level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0{,}05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often specified before the test. The rule\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\le\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontrols the type I error <strong>under the valid null hypothesis and\ntest assumptions<\/strong>, not the probability of error <em>for this\nparticular significant result<\/em>. When several unplanned tests are\nperformed, the risk of at least one false alarm increases without\ncorrection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>AI and automatic classification:<\/strong> Prior\nprobabilities, sensitivity and false alarm rates determine how\ninformative positive results are.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Software benchmarks:<\/strong> Expected value and dispersion\ndistinguish typical running times from unstable outliers; appropriate\nsampling design prevents false certainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Robotics and sensor fusion:<\/strong> Measurement noise can\nbe modelled as a random variable; its distribution determines how\nuncertain a position estimate is.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Simulation:<\/strong> Artificial data are generated from\ntheoretical distributions to estimate possible system states and\nrisks.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A distribution is a <strong>model assumption<\/strong>, not an\nautomatic finding. Real measurements may be dependent, skewed or\nunrepresentative.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Conditional probabilities must not be interchanged:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerally have entirely different values.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A mean does not describe a guaranteed individual output; a small\nsample can fluctuate considerably.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Correlation does not establish causation. In particular, third\nvariables must be considered in observational data.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In continuous models, <strong>density values<\/strong> must be\ndistinguished from <strong>probabilities over intervals<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>M1-L8 examines how such and other mathematical models are computed\n<strong>on a real computer<\/strong>: with finite precision,\napproximation methods, error control and simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M1-L8 – University: Numerical and computational mathematics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Core university subject · Numerical\nmathematics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Mathematical formulas work conceptually with exact numbers. A\ncomputer, by contrast, has a finite number of bits, limited memory and\nfinite computing time. Many results must therefore be obtained\n<strong>approximately<\/strong>. Numerical mathematics develops methods\nfor doing this and investigates when the calculated approximation can be\ntrusted. It distinguishes between two problems: a mathematical problem\nmay be sensitive even to small changes in its inputs; in addition, the\nchosen algorithm itself may amplify errors. This distinction is central\nto robust software, simulation and AI.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine that you are looking for the square root of two. The exact\nnumber has infinitely many non-repeating decimal places; a computer can\nstore only a finite representation. It must approach the solution step\nby step or use a built-in algorithm. What matters is not simply that\nmany decimal places appear, but <strong>how large the actual error may\nstill be<\/strong>. A good numerical method can explain and measure its\napproximation and provide safeguards against rounding problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before they are used<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\ast}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x star’<\/td>\n<td>Exact solution sought<\/td>\n<td>Often an optimal solution in optimisation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x sub k’<\/td>\n<td>Approximation after the k-th step<\/td>\n<td>General sequence index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘k’<\/td>\n<td>Iteration number<\/td>\n<td>Also a constant in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘f of x’<\/td>\n<td>Function whose root is sought<\/td>\n<td>General function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘f prime of x’<\/td>\n<td>Derivative for Newton’s method<\/td>\n<td>Local rate of change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘epsilon’<\/td>\n<td>A chosen error tolerance<\/td>\n<td>An arbitrarily small positive number in formal limits<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≈<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘approximately equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Approximate rather than exact value<\/td>\n<td>General approximation symbol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert e\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘absolute value of e’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative magnitude of an error<\/td>\n<td>Elsewhere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes Euler’s number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec r\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘norm of r’<\/td>\n<td>Magnitude of an error or residual vector<\/td>\n<td>Different norms are possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘kappa of A’<\/td>\n<td>Condition number of a matrix in a chosen norm<\/td>\n<td>Kappa may denote an arbitrary parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘delta x’<\/td>\n<td>Change in or perturbation of an input<\/td>\n<td>Finite difference in differentiation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘big O of h to the power p’<\/td>\n<td>Order of error for a small step size h<\/td>\n<td>Computer science: similar notation for running-time growth<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec x=\\vec b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘matrix A times vector x equals vector b’<\/td>\n<td>System of linear equations<\/td>\n<td>Vectors and matrices from M1-L5–M1-L6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec r=\\vec b-A\\cdot\\vec x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘r equals b minus A times x’<\/td>\n<td>Residual of an approximation<\/td>\n<td>Not identical to the solution error<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MC<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘Monte Carlo’<\/td>\n<td>Estimation by random sampling<\/td>\n<td>Not a mathematical symbol in the strict sense<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘h’<\/td>\n<td>Positive step size in a discretisation<\/td>\n<td>Also a variable for height<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘p’<\/td>\n<td>Positive order of error in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Probability in M1-L7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n’<\/td>\n<td>Number of independent simulations<\/td>\n<td>Also the number of columns in matrices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert A^{-1}\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘norm of the inverse of matrix A’<\/td>\n<td>Amplification factor for a linear residual when the matrix is\ninvertible<\/td>\n<td>The norm must be specified<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The star in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\ast}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis merely a <strong>distinguishing label<\/strong>: it does not mean\nmultiplication. A subscript such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nidentifies the step in an iterative sequence. The norm used – for\ninstance, Euclidean length – must be specified when comparing\nerrors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why a computer calculates differently from calculations on\npaper<\/h2>\n<p>A binary floating-point number stores a sign, a finite sequence of\nsignificant bits and an exponent. Many simple decimal fractions,\nincluding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhave no finite representation in binary. In a common binary\nfloating-point format, for example, <code>0.1 + 0.2<\/code> in a program\nmay produce a value that is not bit-for-bit identical to the stored\napproximation of <code>0.3<\/code>. This does not disprove mathematics;\nit is a consequence of differently rounded machine values.<\/p>\n<p>The order of operations can matter because of rounding. For\nfloating-point numbers, the associative law of ordinary real addition\ndoes not hold in every machine calculation. When values differ greatly\nin magnitude, small summands can be lost entirely. An equally important\nrisk is <strong>cancellation<\/strong> (<em>catastrophic\ncancellation<\/em>), when nearly equal numbers are subtracted, leaving\nfew meaningful digits.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Mini-example of a more stable rearrangement:<\/strong> The\nmathematical expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{x^2+1}-x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be difficult to evaluate accurately for large positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause the two terms are nearly equal. Multiplying by the appropriate\nconjugate factor produces the exactly equivalent expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}+x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt avoids this particular subtraction of nearly equal numbers. In real\nsoftware, overflow when calculating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust also be avoided, for example by using an appropriately robust\nsquare-root/hypotenuse routine. <strong>Limitation:<\/strong> For very\nlarge <strong>negative<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncancellation can now occur in the denominator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{x^2+1}+x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rearranged version is therefore particularly favourable for\n<strong>large positive<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrather than universally superior.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Error, residual and conditioning<\/h2>\n<p>The difference between an approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the exact solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\ast}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called the <strong>solution error<\/strong>; for a scalar quantity, we\nconsider its absolute magnitude:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">absolute error<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{absolute error}=\\lvert x_k-x^*\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The absolute error is the absolute\nvalue of x sub k minus x star.’ In many real problems, the exact\nsolution is unknown; we therefore <strong>cannot calculate the actual\nerror directly<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>For an equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe <strong>residual<\/strong> is the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich has not yet reached zero. A small residual does not necessarily\nimply a small solution error. Example: For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000001<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=0{,}000001\\cdot(x-100)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a residual of only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0001<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0{,}0001<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nalthough the exact root is 100 and the solution error is therefore 100.\nA tiny slope makes root finding sensitive. For an\n<strong>invertible<\/strong> linear system\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec x=\\vec b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec x_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec r=\\vec b-A\\cdot\\vec x_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfy the exact relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec x-\\vec x_k=A^{-1}\\cdot\\vec r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence the bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>≤<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert\\vec x-\\vec x_k\\rVert\\leq\\lVert A^{-1}\\rVert\\cdot\\lVert\\vec r\\rVert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows for compatible vector and induced matrix norms. Without knowing\nor estimating the amplification factor, a small residual provides no\ngeneral error guarantee.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Conditioning<\/strong> describes this sensitivity of the\nmathematical problem to its inputs. <strong>Stability<\/strong> describes\nhow well an algorithm handles unavoidable perturbations and rounding\nerrors. For matrix equations, very large condition numbers are a warning\nsign: even small data errors or rounding errors can lead to markedly\ndifferent solutions. Merely checking the determinant provides no general\nguarantee here. <strong>Numerical counterexample:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1000000<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000001<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(1000000,\\ 0{,}000001)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas determinant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the Euclidean norm, however, the condition number is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1000000<\/mn><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000001<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa(A)=\\frac{1000000}{0{,}000001}=10^{12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA perturbation in the second, small-scale coordinate can therefore be\nsubstantially amplified in relative terms, even though the determinant\nappears unremarkable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nested intervals: safely bracketing a root<\/h2>\n<p>We seek the positive solution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo do this, we consider the function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is continuous. Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave opposite signs, the intermediate value theorem ensures that a root\nlies between 1 and 2.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>bisection method<\/strong> halves this interval and\nretains the half whose endpoints have opposite signs:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Start: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Midpoint: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>→<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Midpoint: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4375<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>→<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Midpoint: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>375<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>375<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>109375<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>→<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>375<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\begin{array}{lll}\n\\text{Start: }[1;2]&amp;f(1)=-1,\\quad f(2)=2&amp;\\\\\n\\text{Midpoint: }1{,}5&amp;f(1{,}5)=0{,}25&amp;\\to[1;1{,}5]\\\\\n\\text{Midpoint: }1{,}25&amp;f(1{,}25)=-0{,}4375&amp;\\to[1{,}25;1{,}5]\\\\\n\\text{Midpoint: }1{,}375&amp;f(1{,}375)=-0{,}109375&amp;\\to[1{,}375;1{,}5]\n\\end{array}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘We halve the interval, evaluate the\nfunction at its midpoint and retain the half in which the sign changes.’\nAfter three halvings, the remaining interval has width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead of its original width of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the exact mathematical model, the root sought still lies within it.\nThe midpoint of the remaining bracket then has an absolute error of at\nmost half the interval width. <strong>This guaranteed enclosure\ninitially assumes exact sign decisions.<\/strong> In an ordinary\nfloating-point implementation, rounding errors may even distort the\ncalculated sign; anyone seeking to guarantee a <em>rigorous<\/em>\nenclosure needs suitable error bounds or outward-rounded interval\narithmetic. A sensible stopping criterion is also needed. Given an\ninitial interval width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b-a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhalvings, the width is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{b-a}{2^k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the error bound for the midpoint is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{b-a}{2^{k+1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<strong>provided<\/strong> that the enclosure remains valid after every\nstep. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[1,2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan error of at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>16<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{16}=0{,}0625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore guaranteed after three steps. The method thus finds a root\nin the interval; uniqueness does not follow from the change of sign\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Newton’s method: obtaining the next approximation from a\ntangent<\/h2>\n<p>Newton’s method replaces the function locally with its tangent and\nuses the tangent’s intersection with the x-axis as the new estimate:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_006_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Tangent iteration for the root of x² minus 2\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A Newton tangent step improves the initial estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the root of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=x_k-\\frac{f(x_k)}{f\\prime(x_k)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The next approximation, x sub k plus\none, is the current approximation, x sub k, minus the function value\ndivided by the derivative at the current approximation.’\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the first derivative, explained in School Mathematics M2-L3–School\nMathematics M2-L4. Division by zero is not allowed; the derivative must\nbe defined and non-zero at the current point.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x)=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=1{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>416666<\/mn><mi>…<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>416666<\/mn><mi>…<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>416666<\/mn><mi>…<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>416666<\/mn><mi>…<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>414215686<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nx_1&amp;=1{,}5-\\frac{{(1{,}5)}^2-2}{2\\cdot1{,}5}=1{,}416666\\ldots\\\\\nx_2&amp;=1{,}416666\\ldots-\\frac{{(1{,}416666\\ldots)}^2-2}{2\\cdot(1{,}416666\\ldots)}\n\\approx1{,}414215686\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The actual positive root is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>414213562<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1{,}414213562<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a good starting value, Newton’s method is considerably faster than\nbisection here. However, Newton’s method does <strong>not<\/strong>\nguarantee convergence from every starting value. It can jump far away,\nbecome cyclic or fail because of an unsuitable derivative. In robust\napplications, numerical libraries therefore often combine fast steps\nwith safeguarded intervals. <strong>Boundary case:<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe derivative at the starting value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis zero, but the root has already been reached. A program must first\ncheck whether the residual is sufficiently small before blindly dividing\nby the derivative. Quadratic local convergence of Newton’s method can\nonly be expected under suitable conditions on smoothness, the starting\nvalue and the root being simple.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Orders of error, linear systems and simulations<\/h2>\n<p>In approximation formulas, the notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates that the magnitude of the error under consideration is, for\nsufficiently small positive step sizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbounded by a constant times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The error is of order h to the power p.’\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhalving the step size can approximately quarter the leading error in the\nasymptotic regime. This does not hold indefinitely: rounding errors may\ndominate for very small steps.<\/p>\n<p>In practice, large linear systems\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot\\vec x=\\vec b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare generally <strong>not<\/strong> solved by explicitly calculating the\ninverse matrix. Instead, methods include Gaussian elimination with\npivoting, LU or QR decompositions and, for suitable large sparse\nmatrices, iterative methods. The choice depends on the matrix structure,\nconditioning, memory and accuracy requirements.<\/p>\n<p>Additional sources of error matter in simulations: an approximate\nmodel equation, a discrete time-stepping scheme, random fluctuations and\nlimited computational precision. A <strong>Monte Carlo\nsimulation<\/strong> averages many random trials. Typically, for\nindependent, identically distributed trials with finite variance, the\nstandard error of a sample mean decreases in proportion to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{\\sqrt n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of trials. Under these assumptions, four times as many\ntrials approximately halve the standard error – not the overall model\nerror. This simple standard-error rule does not hold in general for\ndependent samples or infinite variance. Likewise,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only an <strong>asymptotic<\/strong> bound, not an error value for\nevery arbitrary step size.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>AI training:<\/strong> Weights are changed iteratively;\nrounding, poorly scaled gradients and ill-conditioned problems affect\nthe computation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Robotics:<\/strong> Motion and control equations must be\napproximated using real measurements, limited computing time and\nnumerical integrators.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Data processing:<\/strong> Matrix decompositions, stable norm\ncalculations and error estimates guard against seemingly plausible but\nunreliable results.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Simulation and agent planning:<\/strong> Approximation\nmethods allow numerous state variants to be evaluated, provided that\ntheir assumptions and errors are controlled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common misconceptions<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Many decimal places are <strong>not proof<\/strong> of accuracy. A\nsuitable error model or a reliable bound is needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A small residual does not necessarily imply a small solution error,\nespecially for ill-conditioned problems.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A symbolically exact formula is not automatically the most\nnumerically favourable formula on a computer.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A fast iteration can fail. A slower method with guaranteed nested\nintervals is often a useful safeguard.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Simulation results inherit the weaknesses of the input data and\nmodel assumptions. More computing power does not eliminate a systematic\nmodelling error.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p>M1-L9 deals with ordinary differential equations: how can changes\nover time in stocks, motion and feedback be formulated and investigated?\nCalculus, linear algebra and numerical computation come together\ndirectly in that lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M1-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M1-L9 – Ordinary differential equations: Dynamics, stability and feedback control<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor’s: mathematical foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> University – typical area of mathematical\nstudy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Many systems are described not only by their current state, but also\nby how quickly that state is changing at the moment. How quickly does an\nobject cool down? How does a stock change over time? How does a robot\nrespond when its controller constantly makes small corrections? A\n<strong>differential equation<\/strong> specifies a relationship between\nan unknown function and one or more of its derivatives. We explore the\nunderlying idea, some typical models and their limitations, without\nreplacing a full university course on solution methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>In an ordinary equation, we look for a number; in an <strong>ordinary\ndifferential equation<\/strong> (abbreviated ODE), we usually look for an\nentire trajectory over time. Rather than specifying the temperature of\nan object separately at each instant, we formulate a law of change.\nTogether with a known initial value, this law allows us to determine a\ntime-dependent function under suitable conditions.<\/p>\n<p>Here, ‘ordinary’ means that the unknown function depends on\n<strong>one independent variable<\/strong>, often time. If it depends on\nseveral spatial and temporal variables and involves partial derivatives,\nwe speak of a <strong>partial differential equation<\/strong> (preview in\nM2-L8).<\/p>\n<p>A first-order ODE uses the first derivative. A second-order equation\nmay also contain a second derivative; in mechanics, for example, this\ndescribes acceleration. An <strong>initial value problem<\/strong>\ncombines the law of change with the initial state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other possible uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘tee’<\/td>\n<td>Time as the independent variable<\/td>\n<td>General parameter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y of t’<\/td>\n<td>Unknown state function at time t<\/td>\n<td>Any function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime}(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘d y by d t’ / ‘y prime of t’<\/td>\n<td>Instantaneous rate of change with respect to time<\/td>\n<td>Derivative with respect to another variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm d^2y}{\\mathrm dt^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘second derivative of y with respect to t’<\/td>\n<td>Change in the rate of change, e.g. acceleration<\/td>\n<td>Not an ordinary square of y<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘kay’<\/td>\n<td>Here, a <strong>positive<\/strong> rate per unit time<\/td>\n<td>General constant or iteration index<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y nought’<\/td>\n<td>Specified initial value<\/td>\n<td>First term of a sequence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Euler’s number’<\/td>\n<td>Base of the natural exponential function, approximately 2.71828<\/td>\n<td>Often an error or event variable in code<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-kt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘e to the power of minus k times t’<\/td>\n<td>Exponential decay factor over time<\/td>\n<td>Exponential terms can also describe growth<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘f of t and y’<\/td>\n<td>General law of change<\/td>\n<td>Multivariate function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘aitch’<\/td>\n<td>Step size in numerical computation<\/td>\n<td>Height in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n/\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y / t subscript n’<\/td>\n<td>Numerical approximation at the nth time step<\/td>\n<td>General terms of a sequence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≈<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘approximately equal to’<\/td>\n<td>Rounded number, not an exact identity<\/td>\n<td>Approximation in general<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\ast}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x star’<\/td>\n<td>Equilibrium state<\/td>\n<td>Also the unknown exact solution in M1-L8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘natural logarithm’<\/td>\n<td>Inverse of the exponential function to base\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Already introduced in School Mathematics M1-L19 (German-language\ncourse)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A simple law of change<\/h2>\n<p>If a quantity decreases more quickly the larger it currently is, a\nvery common model arises:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-k\\cdot y(t)\\qquad\\text{with }k&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The derivative of y with respect to\ntime t equals minus k times the current value of y; k is positive.’ The\nminus sign means that the quantity falls when it is positive.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontrols the rate. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis expressed in hours,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the unit ‘per hour’; the right-hand side therefore has the same unit\nas the rate of change on the left.<\/p>\n<p>The function satisfying this law and taking the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat time zero is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=y_0e^{-kt}\\qquad\\text{with }y(0)=y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The state at time t is the initial\nvalue y nought times e to the power of minus k times t. Initially, the\nstate equals y nought.’ This is <strong>one possible solution<\/strong>\nto this particular law of change; under the stated assumptions, it is\nthe unique solution for the specified initial value. Without an initial\ncondition, there would be many solutions with different starting values.\nHere the right-hand side\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-k\\cdot y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous everywhere and Lipschitz continuous with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe solution is therefore unique for every real initial value. For more\ngeneral equations, specifying an initial value alone is\n<strong>not<\/strong> a guarantee of uniqueness.<\/p>\n<p>Why does the function satisfy the equation? Its derivative is the\nsame exponential function again, this time multiplied by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt therefore satisfies the law of change. We know the concepts from\nderivatives (School Mathematics M2-L3) and the exponential function\n(School Mathematics M1-L17 / M1-L19, German-language course).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully explained mini-example: Cooling<\/h2>\n<p>An object initially at 80 °C is in an environment with a constant\ntemperature of 20 °C. We consider the <strong>temperature difference\nrelative to the surroundings<\/strong>. Initially it is 60 °C. We choose\na cooling rate of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0{,}1\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(read aloud as ‘zero point one per minute’) for our model; time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in minutes. Thus the product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\cdot t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the exponent is <strong>dimensionless<\/strong>. The simplified\nnotation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0{,}1\\cdot t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the following numerical formula applies only when the numerical value\nin minutes is substituted for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/U_MAT_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Temperature curve T(t) equals 20 plus 60 times e to the power of minus 0.1t. It starts at 80 degrees Celsius and approaches the ambient temperature of 20 degrees Celsius.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The modelled temperature drops from 80 °C and\napproaches the ambient temperature of 20 °C.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Exponential cooling model with a constant ambient temperature of 20\n°C.<\/p>\n<p>The absolute temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore given by:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(t)=20\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}+60\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\cdot e^{-0{,}1t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The temperature after t minutes is\ntwenty degrees Celsius plus sixty degrees Celsius times e to the power\nof minus zero point one times t.’ The exponential function operates on\nthe <strong>difference relative to the surroundings<\/strong>, not on the\nabsolute temperature in degrees Celsius.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initially\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the temperature is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20+60=80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After ten minutes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3679<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx0{,}3679<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3679<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>07<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(10)\\approx20+60\\cdot0{,}3679\\approx42{,}07\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As time increases indefinitely, the temperature approaches 20 °C\nwithout falling below it in the idealised model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The model is a simplified form of Newton’s law of cooling. Among\nother things, it assumes a constant ambient temperature and an\napproximately constant heat transfer coefficient. The example is not a\nuniversal natural law for every temperature change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Growth, capacity limits and equilibria<\/h2>\n<p>Using a <strong>positive<\/strong> sign instead of the minus sign\ngives the growth law\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=k\\cdot y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe quantity then increases more quickly the larger it is. This models\ncertain unrestricted processes, but is often unrealistic in the long\nrun.<\/p>\n<p>If a population or stock is to approach a capacity limit, a logistic\nmodel can be used. Before the formula, we need two additional symbols:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the positive intrinsic growth rate and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the positive capacity or saturation limit.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mi>y<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=r\\cdot y\\cdot\\left(1-\\frac{y}{K}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The rate of change of y with respect to\ntime equals the growth rate r times the population y times one minus the\npopulation divided by capacity K.’ As long as the population is between\nzero and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe growth rate is positive. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit becomes zero; a population above\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases in the model. Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the same unit, the factor in brackets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mi>y<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\frac yK<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis dimensionless;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of inverse time.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>equilibrium<\/strong> is a state in which the rate of\nchange is zero. In the logistic model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare equilibria. For positive initial populations near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a stabilising effect; for small positive populations, however,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unstable. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a <strong>positive initial population<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe can even solve the equation explicitly. The exponential function is\napplied only to a dimensionless expression:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> and <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\frac{K}{1+\\frac{K-y_0}{y_0}\\cdot e^{-rt}}\\qquad\\text{for }t\\geq0\\text{ and }y_0&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘Capacity K divided by one plus the\ninitial difference from capacity relative to the initial population,\nmultiplied by the exponential decay factor.’ For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0{,}2\\,\\mathrm{day}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>100<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=\\frac{100}{1+4}=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>93<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{\\ln(4)}{0{,}2}\\approx6{,}93<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-0{,}2t}=\\frac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>100<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\frac{100}{1+4\\cdot\\frac14}=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever, the constant zero function is a solution; the displayed\nfractional formula must not be used in this case because it divides by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEquilibrium analysis describes the direction of possible changes; it\ndoes not establish a biological prediction without suitable data and\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feedback and stability<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a controller that continually reduces the error between the\ndesired and actual states. We denote this error by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand again choose a positive control gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-k\\cdot x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The rate of change of the error with\nrespect to time is minus k times the current error.’ A positive error\ndecreases; a negative error also approaches zero, provided the model\napplies. The equilibrium state is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\ast}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is <strong>asymptotically stable<\/strong> in the continuous model\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nsmall deviations decay over time.<\/p>\n<p>Realistic feedback control loops may involve inertia, time delays,\nnoise, actuator limits and further state variables. Excessive corrective\naction can then lead to oscillations or instability. The simple model is\nan introduction, not a complete robot controller.<\/p>\n<p>With several state variables, we can model a linear system with\nmatrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand state vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm d\\vec x}{\\mathrm dt}=A\\cdot\\vec x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalues of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(M1-L6) help us understand stability: if all the real parts of the\neigenvalues of a <strong>time-invariant linear system<\/strong> are\nnegative, the zero state is asymptotically stable. This establishes a\nconnection with linear algebra; it is not a substitute for analysing\nnonlinear systems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When no convenient solution exists: Euler’s method<\/h2>\n<p>Many ODEs have no simple closed-form solution. We then calculate the\ntrajectory step by step. For a law of change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=f(t,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand step size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe <strong>explicit Euler method<\/strong> is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nt_{n+1}&amp;=t_n+h\\\\\ny_{n+1}&amp;=y_n+h f(t_n,y_n)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> ‘The next time is the current time plus\nthe step size. The next state value is the current state value plus the\nstep size times the currently estimated rate of change.’ This method\nassumes that the rate remains approximately constant during the short\nstep.<\/p>\n<p>For the cooling temperature difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=60\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=-0{,}1\\cdot y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na time step of <strong>one minute<\/strong> gives:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_1=60+1\\cdot(-0{,}1\\cdot60)=54\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute temperature after one minute is therefore approximately 74\n°C. The exact model value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>74<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>29<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20+60\\cdot e^{-0{,}1}\\approx74{,}29\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe discrepancy arises from time discretisation.<\/p>\n<p>After ten steps of one minute each, Euler’s method gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>92<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20+60\\cdot\\left(0{,}9\\right)^{10}\\approx40{,}92\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe analytical model solution gave approximately 42.07 °C after ten\nminutes. For this problem, smaller steps typically improve the\napproximation but increase computational cost. <strong>Not every step\nsize is stable:<\/strong> for the model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}=-k\\cdot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Euler formula gives exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=(1-h\\cdot k)\\cdot x_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe repeated approximation tends asymptotically to zero only when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert1-h\\cdot k\\rvert&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthat is,\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;h\\cdot k&lt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;h\\cdot k\\leq1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na positive initial error does not change sign; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1&lt;h\\cdot k&lt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndamped oscillations with alternating signs arise. At the boundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\cdot k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe error alternates between two signs without decaying; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\cdot k&gt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits magnitude increases.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Check example:<\/strong> starting with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0{,}1\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=15\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-h\\cdot k=-0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nEuler’s method yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=-5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=2{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n– the approximation oscillates about zero although the exact solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\cdot e^{-0{,}1t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains positive. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=25\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Euler’s method yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>22<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">22{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nan artificially unstable trajectory. This is a\n<strong>numerical<\/strong> instability, not an instability of the\nunderlying continuous model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Robotics and control:<\/strong> Joint movements, velocity,\ninertia and correction signals are described as state trajectories.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Server and agent systems:<\/strong> Changes in stocks,\nbacklogs or resource consumption can be studied using suitable\ncontinuous approximation models.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Simulation:<\/strong> Numerical ODE solvers enable\npredictions about dynamical systems even when no simple closed-form\nsolution exists.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>AI and optimisation:<\/strong> Continuous learning and\nadaptation models build on gradients and differential equations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, limitations and common misconceptions<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A law of change alone usually does not determine a unique trajectory\nover time; initial values or other conditions are necessary.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A differential equation is <strong>a model<\/strong>. Measured data\nmay contain noise, abrupt events and parameter changes that the model\ndoes not include.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The mathematical existence and uniqueness of a solution depend on\nassumptions such as sufficient regularity of the right-hand side; they\nare not automatically guaranteed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A numerical approximation is not an exact solution; discretisation\nerrors occur in addition to rounding errors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The stability of a continuous system does not guarantee stability\nfor an arbitrarily coarse numerical discretisation. Even a stable step\nsize for the linear test model is not a universal error or stability\nguarantee for other ODEs; for general right-hand sides, local smoothness\nor Lipschitz conditions and, where appropriate, adaptive steps must be\nexamined.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next unit<\/h2>\n<p>M1-L9 completes the planned university foundations block. M2-L1 moves\non to <strong>discrete mathematics<\/strong>: instead of continuous\ntrajectories over time, it considers nodes, edges, finite structures and\nstate spaces that can be searched algorithmically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
